\subsection{Sensitivity-weighted simulation}

\label{subsec:app-theory}

We first design the effective transfer function so that its value at the evaluation point generates the desired Schur complement.

Rescaling the internal equations preserves this target but changes the transfer function away from the evaluation point, and hence its group delay.

Choose positive sector scales and define

\begin{align}

T_Q=\sum_{i\ge1}s_iQ_i,

\quad s_0=1.

\end{align}

The identity $BT_Q(T_QKT_Q)^{-1}T_QB^\dagger=BK^{-1}B^\dagger$ shows that this congruence preserves the effective Hamiltonian.

\cref{lem:app-loads} proves that taking each sector scale equal to its response bound minimizes the stated componentwise upper bounds on the delay-weighted catalyst bound.

This balances the two ends of every coupling; it makes no claim to minimize the actual group-delay operator over all realizations.

The congruent matrix and its elimination map are

\begin{align}

\widetilde{\mathcal H}= \left(\begin{array}{c|c}A&BT_Q

\\

\hline

T_QB^\dagger&T_QKT_Q\end{array}\right),

\quad V=\begin{pmatrix}I_{\mathcal P}

\\

T_Q^{-1}X\end{pmatrix}.

\label{eq:app-congruence}

\end{align}

The identity $(T_QKT_Q)^{-1}=T_Q^{-1}K^{-1}T_Q^{-1}$ shows that the effective Hamiltonian remains $H_{\mathrm{eff}}$.

A diagonal component acquires normalization $\widetilde\lambda_j=s_i^2\lambda_j$, whereas a coupling acquires $\widetilde\lambda_j=s_is_k\lambda_j$.

In both cases the rescaled component $\widetilde H_j$ satisfies $\widetilde H_j/\widetilde\lambda_j=H_j/\lambda_j$ on its support.

We can therefore implement the rescaling by changing known junction coefficients while using the original $\Be{H_j/\lambda_j}$ and signal input with ancillas initialized to zero.

\cref{lem:app-native} realizes these rescaled elimination equations by closing the internal public coordinates of a Cayley transformation.

\Needspace{8\baselineskip}

\begin{lemma}[Schur complement from internal feedback]

\label{lem:app-native}

For complex markers $\Bz=(z_1,\ldots,z_m)$ in the open unit polydisk, define the supported load

\begin{align}

\mathsf M(\Bz)=\sum_j\left[ \widetilde\lambda_j\frac{1-z_j^2}{1+z_j^2}\Pi_j -i\frac{2z_j}{1+z_j^2}\widetilde H_j\right].

\label{eq:app-load}

\end{align}

Let $\mathscr S(\Bz)$ be its Schur complement on $\mathcal P$.

For every $u>0$, we can connect the component oracles to a known unitary system matrix and feed the public output of each internal sector $Q_i$ back to its input.

The active-space response is

\begin{align}

F_u(\Bz)=(I-u\mathscr S(\Bz)) (I+u\mathscr S(\Bz))^{-1}.

\label{eq:app-cell}

\end{align}

The load $\mathscr S$ is analytic and accretive in the polydisk: $\appRe\mathscr S=(\mathscr S+\mathscr S^\dagger)/2\succeq0$.

It continues analytically to $\Bz=\boldsymbol{1}$, where $\mathscr S=-iH_{\mathrm{eff}}$.

At $\Bz=\boldsymbol{1}$, the active-space input and output are $\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y}$.

Together with the internal feedback amplitude $\boldsymbol{g}$, they obey

\begin{align}

\boldsymbol{g}=T_Q^{-1}X\frac{\boldsymbol{x}+\boldsymbol{y}}{2},

\quad \boldsymbol{y}=(I+iuH_{\mathrm{eff}})(I-iuH_{\mathrm{eff}})^{-1}\boldsymbol{x}.

\label{eq:app-selfconsistency}

\end{align}

\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}

We couple the signal inputs with ancillas initialized to zero to their supported public sectors through the known map $L_u=(\sqrt{u\widetilde\lambda_j}\,\Pi_j(\langle0|_j\otimes I))_j$.

Its domain is the direct sum of the full oracle spaces.

Define $R_u=(I+L_uL_u^\dagger)^{-1}$ and the system matrix

\begin{align}

S_u=\left(\begin{array}{c|c} 2R_u-I&-2iR_uL_u

\\

\hline

2L_u^\dagger R_u&i(I-2L_u^\dagger R_uL_u) \end{array}\right).

\label{eq:app-junction}

\end{align}

Block multiplication with $R_u+R_uL_uL_u^\dagger=I$ verifies unitarity.

Connect private port $j$ through $-z_j\Be{H_j/\lambda_j}$.

The involution property gives

\begin{align}

&\bigl(I-iz_j\Be{H_j/\lambda_j}\bigr) \bigl(I+iz_j\Be{H_j/\lambda_j}\bigr)^{-1}

\\

&

\quad=\frac{1-z_j^2}{1+z_j^2}I -i\frac{2z_j}{1+z_j^2}\Be{H_j/\lambda_j}.

\end{align}

Compressing by $(|0\rangle_j\otimes I)\Pi_j$ and summing yields \cref{eq:app-load}, so the response before closing the internal sectors is $(I-u\mathsf M)(I+u\mathsf M)^{-1}$.

Each compressed Cayley transform has positive definite real part on its support for $|z_j|<1$.

Invertibility of $K$ implies that these supports cover the internal space.

Hence $\mathsf M_{QQ}$ is invertible throughout the polydisk.

Equating the input and output on each internal sector and eliminating them gives \cref{eq:app-cell}.

To bound the resulting load, define $V(\Bz)=\binom{I}{-\mathsf M_{QQ}^{-1}\mathsf M_{QP}}$.

The internal rows of $\mathsf M(\Bz)V(\Bz)$ vanish, which implies

\begin{align}

\appRe\mathscr S(\Bz) =V(\Bz)^\dagger\appRe\mathsf M(\Bz)V(\Bz) \succeq0.

\label{eq:app-energy-identity}

\end{align}

An operator is accretive when its Hermitian part is positive semidefinite.

The identity above proves this property for the effective load; the internal inverse also proves analyticity.

Accretivity explains why the Cayley transfer function is contractive: for any vector $\boldsymbol{v}$ and $u>0$, $\|(I+u\mathscr S)\boldsymbol{v}\|^2 -\|(I-u\mathscr S)\boldsymbol{v}\|^2 =4u\boldsymbol{v}^\dagger(\operatorname{Re}\mathscr S)\boldsymbol{v}\ge0$.

The inverse exists since the real part of the denominator is bounded below by the identity.

This proves contractivity without diagonalizing the noncommuting component operators.

At $\boldsymbol{1}$, the open response is $(I+iu\widetilde{\mathcal H})(I-iu\widetilde{\mathcal H})^{-1}$.

Write the closed internal input and output as the same vector $\boldsymbol{g}$.

Multiplying by $I-iu\widetilde{\mathcal H}$ gives $T_QKT_Q\boldsymbol{g}=-T_QB^\dagger(\boldsymbol{x}+\boldsymbol{y})/2$.

Solving this equation and substituting into the active-space row proves \cref{eq:app-selfconsistency}.

\end{proof}

\cref{eq:app-selfconsistency} identifies the internal solve performed by the IIR: its feedback amplitude is the full coupled response to the mean active-space input and output.

Closing all sectors uses only invertibility of $K$.

Successive Schur complements give the same answer when their intermediate pivots exist, since they eliminate the same equations.

Simultaneous closure therefore includes excursions between all internal sectors in a single active-space response.

\cref{lem:app-loads} balances the component loads so that the catalyst weight follows the response bound.

\begin{lemma}[Balanced response-weighted normalization]

\label{lem:app-loads}

For every component $j$,

\begin{align}

-\left.\partial_{z_j}\mathscr S\right|_{\Bz=\boldsymbol1} =\widetilde\lambda_j V^\dagger\Pi_jV\succeq0.

\label{eq:app-port-action}

\end{align}

The choice $s_i=\varrho_i$ minimizes, simultaneously for all couplings, the upper bounds obtained from the given $\varrho_i$.

For positive integer delays $r_1,\ldots,r_m$, define $D_{\boldsymbol{r}}=\sum_j r_j\widetilde\lambda_j\Pi_j$.

Then

\begin{align}

0\preceq V^\dagger D_{\boldsymbol{r}}V =-\left.\frac{d}{dz}\mathscr S(z^{r_1},\ldots,z^{r_m})\right|_{z=1} \preceq\left(\sum_jr_j\lambda_j \chi_j\right)I_{\mathcal P}.

\label{eq:app-delayed-action}

\end{align}

\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}

Differentiate the load in \cref{eq:app-load}.

At $z_j=1$, the identity coefficient has derivative $-1$ and the matrix coefficient has derivative zero.

Differentiating the Schur complement sandwiches $-\widetilde\lambda_j\Pi_j$ between the elimination map and its adjoint, proving \cref{eq:app-port-action}.

For the scale optimization, write the sector response maps as $X_i=Q_iX $ for $i\ge1$ and $X_0=I_{\mathcal P}$.

A diagonal component contributes $\lambda_jX_i^\dagger X_i$ for every $s_i$.

A coupling between $i$ and $k$ contributes

\begin{align}

\lambda_j\left[\frac{s_k}{s_i}X_i^\dagger X_i +\frac{s_i}{s_k}X_k^\dagger X_k\right] \preceq\lambda_j\left[\frac{s_k}{s_i}\varrho_i^2 +\frac{s_i}{s_k}\varrho_k^2\right]I.

\label{eq:app-balancing}

\end{align}

The bracket is minimized at $s_k/s_i=\varrho_k/\varrho_i$, where its value is $2\varrho_i\varrho_k$ by the arithmetic--geometric mean inequality.

Setting $s_i=\varrho_i$ attains this minimum on every edge, including those incident to $P$.

The chain rule for $z_j=z^{r_j}$ and summation with coefficients $r_j$ give \cref{eq:app-delayed-action}.

The argument uses no commutativity assumption on the component matrices.

\end{proof}

We use the balanced choice $T_Q=\sum_{i\ge1}\varrho_iQ_i$ from now on.

The junction normalization is then $\widetilde\lambda_j=\lambda_j\chi_j$ for a diagonal component and $\widetilde\lambda_j=\lambda_j\chi_j/2$ for a coupling.

The latter contributes twice its junction normalization to the bound on the active-space response in \cref{eq:app-delayed-action}.

The derivative bound controls how the active-space response changes with the markers.

Finite-circuit construction also needs a neighborhood in which the internal inverse exists.

For a chosen schedule, let $r_*=\max_jr_j$, set $D_{\boldsymbol{r},Q}=QD_{\boldsymbol{r}}Q$, and define the weighted inverse norm

\begin{align}

\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}}=\left\| D_{\boldsymbol{r},Q}^{1/2}(T_QKT_Q)^{-1}D_{\boldsymbol{r},Q}^{1/2} \right\|.

\label{eq:app-tau}

\end{align}

This quantity measures the stability of the internal equations in the same weighted norm used by the load.

Bounding it by the given $\kappa_j$ gives the analytic neighborhood in \cref{lem:app-cap}, which controls dispersion and truncation in the clock theorem.

\begin{lemma}[Local analyticity with explicit conditioning]

\label{lem:app-cap}

Let $t\ge0$ and let $\widehat\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}}$ be a known uniform upper bound on \cref{eq:app-tau}.

After substituting $z_j=z^{r_j}$, write $\mathscr S(z)=\mathscr S(z^{r_1},\ldots,z^{r_m})$.

The response $e^{-t\mathscr S(z)}$ has norm at most one for $|z|<1$.

It is analytic on an open neighborhood of the closed disk $|z-1|\le\zeta$, where

\begin{align}

\zeta= \frac{1}{64(r_*+\widehat\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}})},

\quad \|e^{-t\mathscr S(z)}\| \le |z|^{3t\sum_j r_j\lambda_j \chi_j}

\quad (|z|\ge1,\ |z-1|\le\zeta).

\label{eq:app-cap}

\end{align}

Condition \cref{eq:app-kappa} implies $\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}}\le\max_j\kappa_jr_j$.

In particular, the smaller radius $[128\max_j\kappa_jr_j]^{-1}$ is valid.

\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}

We estimate changes in the load in the logarithmic coordinate $z=e^w$.

Restrict to its active support, on whose complement the active-space transformation is the identity.

Within this proof write $D=D_{\boldsymbol{r}}$ and regard the load as a function of $w$:

\begin{align}

\mathsf M(w)=-\sum_j\widetilde\lambda_j\tanh(r_jw)\Pi_j -i\sum_j\operatorname{sech}(r_jw)\widetilde H_j.

\label{eq:app-log-load}

\end{align}

For $|w|\le1/(16r_*)$, we have $\|D^{-1/2}\mathsf M'(w)D^{-1/2}\|\le2$.

The bound is independent of the number of components: the maps $\sqrt{r_j\widetilde\lambda_j}\Pi_jD^{-1/2}$ form an isometry when stacked, so each weighted sum is a compression of a direct-sum contraction.

The two scalar derivative coefficients have combined magnitude less than $2$ on this disk.

Integrating from zero therefore gives

\begin{align}

\|D^{-1/2}[\mathsf M(w)+i\widetilde{\mathcal H}]D^{-1/2}\| \le2|w|.

\label{eq:app-load-perturbation}

\end{align}

Since the load is anti-Hermitian on the imaginary axis, integration in the real direction also gives $-\appRe\mathsf M(u+i\theta)\preceq2uD$ for $u\ge0$ in the disk.

A Neumann series in the weighted load perturbation shows that the internal inverse is analytic when $2\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}}|w|<1$, with weighted norm at most $\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}}/(1-2\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}}|w|)$.

Let $V(w)=\binom{I}{-\mathsf M_{QQ}(w)^{-1}\mathsf M_{QP}(w)}$.

Subtract the internal equations at $w$ and at zero, where $V(0)=V$.

Applying \cref{eq:app-load-perturbation} to the residual and \cref{eq:app-delayed-action} to $V$ gives

\begin{align}

\|D^{1/2}[V(w)-V]\| \le\frac{2\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}}|w|}{1-2\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}}|w|} \sqrt{\sum_jr_j\lambda_j \chi_j}.

\label{eq:app-return-perturbation}

\end{align}

On the neighborhood in \cref{eq:app-cap}, $|w|\le2\zeta$, so this change is small enough that $\|D^{1/2}V(w)\|\le(8/7)\sqrt{\sum_jr_j\lambda_j \chi_j}$.

Combining this estimate with \cref{eq:app-energy-identity} yields

\begin{align}

-\appRe\mathscr S(e^{u+i\theta}) \preceq\frac{128}{49}u\left(\sum_jr_j\lambda_j \chi_j\right)I \preceq3u\left(\sum_jr_j\lambda_j \chi_j\right)I.

\end{align}

The bound $\|e^Y\|\le e^{\lambda_{\max}(\appRe Y)}$ proves \cref{eq:app-cap} outside the disk.

Inside the disk, accretivity gives norm at most one.

The strict Neumann-series inequality gives an open set containing the stated closed disk on which the function is analytic.

It remains to express its radius in given parameters.

The delay-weighted internal matrix commutes with the sector scaling.

Using the inverse bound $\|K^{-1}\|\le\Delta^{-1}$ gives

\begin{align}

\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}}\le\frac1\Delta\max_{i\ge1} \left[\sum_{j:\Pi_j=Q_i}r_j\lambda_j +\sum_{k\ne i}\ \sum_{j:\Pi_j=Q_i+Q_k} r_j\lambda_j\frac{\varrho_k}{\varrho_i}\right] \le\max_j\kappa_jr_j,

\label{eq:app-scalar-tau}

\end{align}

where \cref{eq:app-kappa} gives the last inequality.

The use of the full coupled inverse is what permits the same bound when different internal sectors interact.

\end{proof}

For $K\succ0$, the operator order can give a sharper sufficient perturbation bound than the scalar bound \cref{eq:app-kappa}:

\begin{align}

\sum_j\frac{\widetilde\lambda_j}{\kappa_j}Q\Pi_jQ \preceq T_QKT_Q,

\quad \kappa_j\ge1.

\label{eq:app-positive-kappa}

\end{align}

Multiplying this inequality by $\max_j\kappa_jr_j$ bounds $D_{\boldsymbol{r},Q}$ term by term.

Congruence by $(T_QKT_Q)^{-1/2}$ then gives $\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}}\le\max_j\kappa_jr_j$ as before.

The subsequent results therefore also apply with \cref{eq:app-positive-kappa}.

This inequality is assumed or proved from the structure of the application before applying the quantum algorithm.

\begin{lemma}[Allocation with upper bounds on the scaled delays]

\label{lem:app-allocation}

Assume nonnegative catalyst-weight bounds and positive query costs.

For parameters satisfying $a_j\ge0$, $C_j>0$, $b_0>0$, and $\kappa_j\ge1$, one has

\begin{subequations}

\label{eq:app-capped-optimum}

\begin{align}

\left(\sum_j\sqrt{C_ja_j}\right)^2+b_0\sum_jC_j\kappa_j&\le\inf_{r_j\in\mathbb N_{>0}} \left(\sum_ja_jr_j+b_0\max_j\kappa_jr_j\right) \sum_j\frac{C_j}{r_j}

\\

&\le4\left[\left(\sum_j\sqrt{C_ja_j}\right)^2 +b_0\sum_jC_j\kappa_j\right].

\end{align}

\end{subequations}

A constructive schedule is obtained from the real numbers

\begin{align}

x_j=\min\left\{\frac1{\kappa_j},\, \sqrt{\frac{b_0C_j}{a_j\sum_kC_k\kappa_k}}\right\},

\quad r_j=\left\lceil\frac{x_j}{\min_kx_k}\right\rceil.

\label{eq:app-capped-delays}

\end{align}

At $a_j=0$, take $x_j=1/\kappa_j$.

\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}

We bound the time and conditioning contributions separately.

Cauchy--Schwarz gives $(\sum_j\sqrt{C_ja_j})^2$ for the first.

For the conditioning contribution, use

\begin{align}

\frac{\max_k\kappa_kr_k}{r_j}\ge\kappa_j

\quad\text{for every }j.

\end{align}

This gives $b_0\sum_jC_j\kappa_j$.

Adding proves the lower bound.

For the constructive upper bound, write $A_0=\sum_j\sqrt{C_ja_j}$ and $B_0=\sum_jC_j\kappa_j$ within this proof.

The real delays in \cref{eq:app-capped-delays} obey

\begin{align}

\sum_ja_jx_j\le\sqrt{b_0/B_0}\,A_0,

\quad \max_j\kappa_jx_j\le1,

\quad \sum_jC_j/x_j\le B_0+\sqrt{B_0/b_0}\,A_0.

\end{align}

Their objective is at most $A_0^2+b_0B_0+2A_0\sqrt{b_0B_0}\le2(A_0^2+b_0B_0)$.

Rescale all delays so that the smallest is one; the objective is unchanged.

Upward integer rounding multiplies the first factor by at most two and can only decrease the second.

This proves the factor-four bound and gives the stated schedule.

\end{proof}

We now apply the unitary Toeplitz lift in \cref{thm:iir-lift} and analytic clock shaping in \cref{thm:iir-analytic-clock-selection} to the response bound.

For fixed delays, let $q_j^{\rm lift}$ count the calls to component $j$ in one forward lift.

\cref{lem:app-cap} gives the analytic radius and exterior growth required by \cref{thm:iir-analytic-clock-selection}, which gives

\begin{align}

N=O\!\left[|t|\sum_jr_j\lambda_j \chi_j +\max_j\kappa_jr_j\log\frac1\epsilon\right],

\quad q_j^{\rm lift}=\left\lfloor\frac{N-1}{r_j}\right\rfloor

\label{eq:app-horizon}

\end{align}

The clock has nuclear norm at most two.

The circuit obtains $\Be{\widetilde G_N/2}$, approximating the desired evolution at normalization two.

Its coefficients depend only on the given bounds, without requiring $X$ or its operator derivative.

For an explicit sufficient horizon, put

\begin{align}

\zeta=[128\max_j\kappa_jr_j]^{-1},

\quad M_{\rm sm}=\left\lceil\frac{2e(1+\zeta)}\zeta\right\rceil,

\quad q_{\rm sm}=\left\lceil\log\frac{49152}{\epsilon}\right\rceil,

\quad d_{\rm sm}=q_{\rm sm}(M_{\rm sm}-1).

\end{align}

Apply \cref{lem:iir-optimal-flat,lem:iir-flat-smoothing} with two coarse blocks, interior exponent zero, and exterior exponent $3|t|\sum_jr_j\lambda_j\chi_j$.

The horizon

\begin{align}

N=2\max\left\{1,\left\lceil3|t|\sum_jr_j\lambda_j\chi_j\right\rceil\right\} +3d_{\rm sm}

\label{eq:app-explicit-N}

\end{align}

follows from \cref{eq:analytic-horizon-exact}.

The flat clock has normalization at most $\sqrt2$; positive smoothing cannot increase it, and known attenuation sets the normalization to two.

Since $\beta\le e^{-1}$ in \cref{eq:analytic-smoothing-parameters-a}, the clock approximation satisfies $\|\widetilde G_N-e^{itH_{\mathrm{eff}}}\|\le3e^{-q_{\rm sm}}\le\epsilon/16384$ before block-encoding normalization.

The encoded-operator error is half this bound at normalization two.

This leaves the error budget needed for finite realization and oblivious amplitude amplification.

The endpoint vectors are computed from the two-block singular vectors and the finite displacement distribution of \cref{eq:analytic-smoothing-random-variable}.

The analytic exponential need not have a finite-dimensional realization.

\cref{lem:app-finite-cascade} constructs a finite Cayley cascade with the required length-$N$ Toeplitz compression.

Approximating this whole block controls the impulse coefficients sampled by the clock; agreement only at $z=1$ would not suffice.

Oblivious amplitude amplification in \cref{lem:iir-cubic} gives time evolution with bounded isometry error.

The total component counts are

\begin{align}

q_j=3\left\lfloor\frac{N-1}{r_j}\right\rfloor.

\label{eq:app-exact-calls}

\end{align}

\cref{lem:iir-cubic} controls both the block-encoding error and leakage for this unitary target.

The reserved error fractions therefore give \cref{eq:app-clean}.

For negative time we reverse the entire positive-time circuit, preserving these counts.

The total query cost is bounded by multiplying \cref{eq:app-horizon} by $\sum_jC_j/r_j$ and the constant cost of oblivious amplitude amplification.

Apply \cref{lem:app-allocation} with $a_j=|t|\lambda_j\chi_j$ and $b_0=\log(2/\epsilon)$.

Since $\max_j\kappa_jr_j\ge1$, the constant in the horizon is absorbed into the precision term.

This proves \cref{eq:app-maincost}, subject to the finite realization established next, and the claimed constant-factor bound for the allocation.

We obtain a finite unitary network by cascading the Cayley transformations of \cref{lem:app-native}.

Sum the rescaled normalizations incident to each sector: \begin{align}

\Lambda_i=\sum_{j:\,Q_i\Pi_j=Q_i}\widetilde\lambda_j,

\quad D_{\boldsymbol{1}}=\sum_i\Lambda_iQ_i.

\end{align} These scalars are computed from the given normalizations.

If $\Lambda_0=0$, the active-space generator vanishes and the identity circuit suffices.

Otherwise the active incident normalization is positive.

Invertibility of the internal block also ensures $\Lambda_i>0$ in every internal sector.

\begin{lemma}[A finite realization accurate on the whole clock]

\label{lem:app-finite-cascade}

Let $t>0$ and let $N$ be chosen as above.

Choose $J$ as the smallest power of two satisfying

\begin{align}

J\ge\max\left\{2,\ 4t\Lambda_0N,\ 64\sqrt{\frac{\mathrm e}{\epsilon}}(4t\Lambda_0N)^{3/2}\right\}.

\label{eq:app-J}

\end{align}

The cascade of $J$ system matrices from \cref{lem:app-native}, with $u=t/(2J)$, has response

\begin{align}

F_J(z)=\left[(I-t\mathscr S(z)/(2J)) (I+t\mathscr S(z)/(2J))^{-1}\right]^J.

\label{eq:app-cascade}

\end{align}

Let $\appT_N[F]$ denote the causal block Toeplitz matrix of an analytic transfer function $F$.

It contains the first $N$ Taylor coefficients in an array with that many block rows and columns.

Then

\begin{align}

\|\appT_N[F_J]-\appT_N[e^{-t\mathscr S}]\| \le\epsilon/4096.

\label{eq:app-toeplitz-approx}

\end{align}

Moreover $\log J=O(\log(1+t\Lambda_0N)+\log(1/\epsilon))$.

\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}

We control the impulse coefficients by bounding the response on a circle inside the unit disk.

Since $\mathsf M(0)=D_{\boldsymbol{1}}$, $\mathscr S(0)=\Lambda_0I_{\mathcal P}$.

Define the analytic contraction $C(z)=(\mathscr S(z)-\Lambda_0I)(\mathscr S(z)+\Lambda_0I)^{-1}$ and $C(0)=0$.

The quotient by the delay variable has a removable singularity at zero.

Applying the maximum modulus principle on circles of radius approaching one gives $\|C(z)\|\le |z|$, the operator Schwarz lemma.

Inverting the Cayley transform then gives

\begin{align}

\|\mathscr S(z)\|\le\Lambda_0\frac{1+|z|}{1-|z|}.

\label{eq:app-carath}

\end{align}

On the chosen interior circle, $|z|=e^{-1/N} $ implies $ \|t\mathscr S(z)\|\le4t\Lambda_0N$.

To approximate the exponential there, use the power-series estimate $\|(I-Z/2)(I+Z/2)^{-1}-e^{-Z}\|\le\|Z\|^3$, valid for accretive $Z$ with $\|Z\|\le1$.

Both the Cayley transform and the exponential are contractions.

Set $Z=t\mathscr S(z)/J$.

Telescoping the cascade factors bounds the error on the circle by $(4t\Lambda_0N)^3/J^2$.

To pass from this transfer-function error to the clock block, use a diagonal rescaling of the clock basis.

For an analytic matrix function, $R_r=\operatorname{diag}(1,r,\ldots,r^{N-1})$, $E_r(z)=E(rz)$, and $R_r\appT_N[E]R_r^{-1}=\appT_N[E_r]$.

The rescaled Toeplitz matrix is a compression of multiplication by the rescaled transfer function on the unit circle.

Its operator norm is therefore bounded by the maximum there, so $\|\appT_N[E]\| \le\|R_r^{-1}\|\,\|R_r\|\sup_{|z|=r}\|E(z)\| =r^{-(N-1)}\sup_{|z|=r}\|E(z)\| $ for $0<r<1$.

At $r=e^{-1/N}$ the prefactor is at most $\mathrm e$.

The choice \cref{eq:app-J} consequently gives \cref{eq:app-toeplitz-approx}.

The endpoint clock maps are contractions, so the block-encoding error is no larger.

Rounding $J$ to a power of two also gives the stated logarithmic bound.

\end{proof}

The number of Cayley transformations can be large, so executing them serially would lose the query advantage.

We instead factor their known system matrix while retaining the full private space.

Let $n$ count qubits in a common physical system register.

Each oracle uses $a_j$ persistent encoding qubits and has controlled gate cost $G_j$, including its representation changes.

We pad these encoding registers to $a_{\max}=\max_ja_j$, with the extra qubits as spectators.

Let $G_{\rm align}$ count a controlled change of basis from the signal inputs with ancillas initialized to zero to the weighted superpositions defined in \cref{eq:app-bright}, together with its inverse, sector tests, and support-basis changes.

Let $s_{\rm supplied}$ be the maximum number of reusable ancillas initialized to $|0\rangle$ used by an oracle or this alignment.

Known changes of basis must be implemented with standard quantum gates, as in \cref{def:resource-accounting}.

\begin{theorem}[Gate, oracle, and workspace counts]

\label{thm:app-gates}

For $N,J,r_j$ as above, define the address qubit count

\begin{align}

b=\max\left\{8,\left\lceil \log_2\left(N+J2^{a_{\max}}\sum_jr_j\right)\right\rceil\right\}.

\label{eq:app-address}

\end{align}

The amplified circuit of \cref{thm:app-main} makes exactly the involution calls in \cref{eq:app-exact-calls}.

With reusable prefix-computation ancillas initialized to $|0\rangle$ it has elementary gate count and total qubit count

\begin{align}

G_{\rm total} &=O\!\left[\sum_jq_jG_j+ N\bigl(G_{\rm align}+(m+1)b\bigr)\right],

\label{eq:app-gates}

\\

n_{\rm total} &=n+b+O\!\left(\log(N+1)+\max\{b,s_{\rm supplied}\}\right)

\\

&=n+O\!\left[a_{\max}+s_{\rm supplied} +\log(2+N+Jmr_*)\right].

\label{eq:app-space}

\end{align}

Elementary gates here include arbitrary one-qubit rotations; their finite-gate-set synthesis is counted in \cref{eq:app-synthesis}.

The cascade length $J$ enters the gate count through the logarithmic width $b$.

A serial execution has depth at most the corresponding fully decomposed gate count.

\end{theorem}

The factorization and circuit proof are in \cref{app:downfolding-circuit}.

When the sectors have explicit labels and their signal inputs with ancillas initialized to zero share a known basis, a direct alignment circuit gives

\begin{align}

G_{\rm align}= O\!\left(m\,[a_{\max}+\log(m+1)+\log(h+1)]\right).

\label{eq:app-explicit-alignment}

\end{align}

There are at most $2m$ sector--component incidences.

Their weighted amplitudes can be prepared by two-level rotations controlled on the sector label and zero encoding ancillas.

Computing and erasing these predicates takes a number of gates linear in the displayed widths.

Given changes of system basis add their actual costs and are the only parts of this alignment that act nontrivially on the physical system register.

The $n$-qubit physical register contains every qubit acted on by the component oracles.

Independent physical subsystems still require their tensor-product space.

The direct-sum packing in \cref{eq:app-carrier} applies to alternative sectors and histories, which accounts for the logarithmic additional address space.

Delaying a component reduces its query frequency but increases the number of known-operation passes through the horizon.

To account for this tradeoff, fix a conservative address-width bound and let $g_{\rm step}$ bound $G_{\rm align}+(m+1)b$.

The leading gate cost is obtained by minimizing

\begin{align}

\left(\sum_j\lambda_j \chi_jr_j\right) \left(g_{\rm step}+\sum_jG_j/r_j\right),

\quad r_j\ge1.

\label{eq:app-gate-objective}

\end{align}

The minimum is within a universal constant of

\begin{align}

g_{\rm step}\sum_j\lambda_j \chi_j+ \left(\sum_j\sqrt{G_j\lambda_j \chi_j}\right)^2

\label{eq:app-gate-leading}

\end{align}

using

\begin{align}

r_j=\left\lceil\max\left\{1, \sqrt{\frac{G_j\sum_k\lambda_k \chi_k}{g_{\rm step}\lambda_j \chi_j}}\right\}\right\rceil.

\label{eq:app-gate-delays}

\end{align}

The constraint $r_j\ge1$ gives the first lower-bound term, and Cauchy--Schwarz gives the second.

For the constructive upper bound, apply $\lceil\max(1,x)\rceil\le2(1+x)$ in the first factor and $r_j\ge\max(1,x)$ in the second.

Their product is at most four times \cref{eq:app-gate-leading}.

To optimize the precision term as well, introduce $R\ge\kappa_jr_j$ for all $j$ and minimize the full objective

\begin{align}

\left(1+|t|\sum_j\lambda_j \chi_jr_j+R\log\frac2\epsilon\right) \left(g_{\rm step}+\sum_jG_j/r_j\right),

\quad r_j\ge1,

\quad R\ge\kappa_jr_j.

\label{eq:app-gate-GP}

\end{align}

With fixed width bounds, this is a geometric program: expanding the objective gives a sum of positive monomials.

Introduce logarithmic coordinates for the delays and their common upper bound, $r_j=e^{y_j}$ and $R=e^Y$.

Each monomial becomes an exponential of an affine function, and the constraints become affine inequalities.

The continuous relaxation can therefore be minimized by convex optimization to a chosen accuracy.

Upward integer rounding changes the cost by at most a universal factor.

The width chosen for $g_{\rm step}$ must cover the resulting $N,J,r_j$, so the optimization accounts for the known operations between delayed oracle calls.

Finite-gate-set synthesis follows \cref{def:resource-accounting}.

A full-unitary implementation error $\xi_j$ in controlled $\Be{H_j/\lambda_j}$ is counted at every occurrence.

Its circuit-error budget is

\begin{align}

\sum_jq_j\xi_j=O(\epsilon).

\label{eq:app-oracle-error}

\end{align}

The hybrid argument bounds the complete-circuit error by this sum.

The costs $G_j$ and scratch widths are evaluated at the corresponding precision.

An error in matrix data encoded by an otherwise exact oracle has a different effect: it changes the internal response and can be bounded using the weights $\chi_j$, as we now show.

\subsubsection{Spectral bounds, correlation weights, and finite resources}

\label{subsubsec:app-spectral}

We now derive the response bound in \cref{cor:app-spectral} and its conditioning and circuit consequences.

Squaring the Hermitian coupling component gives

\begin{align}

H_B^2=\left(\begin{array}{c|c}BB^\dagger&0

\\

\hline

0&B^\dagger B\end{array}\right),

\quad \|H_B\|=\|B\|,

\quad \|X\|=\|K^{-1}B^\dagger\|\le\frac{h_B}{\Delta}.

\label{eq:app-spectral-response-proof}

\end{align}

Thus the bound concerns every active-space input and requires only the given bounds $h_B$ and $\Delta$.

\begin{proof}[Proof of \cref{cor:app-spectral}]

Use the internal scale $T_Q=\varrho I_Q$, with $\varrho=h_B/\Delta$.

The congruent matrix and elimination map are

\begin{align}

\widetilde{\mathcal H} =\left(\begin{array}{c|c}A&\varrho B

\\

\hline

\varrho B^\dagger&\varrho^2K\end{array}\right),

\quad V=\begin{pmatrix}I_P

\\

X/\varrho\end{pmatrix},

\quad \widetilde\lambda_A=\lambda_A,

\quad \widetilde\lambda_B=\varrho\lambda_B,

\quad \widetilde\lambda_K=\varrho^2\lambda_K.

\label{eq:app-spectral-congruence}

\end{align}

Indeed, $(\varrho^2K)^{-1}=\varrho^{-2}K^{-1}$, so eliminating the internal block gives $A-BK^{-1}B^\dagger=H_{\mathrm{eff}}$.

On each support, $\widetilde H_j/\widetilde\lambda_j=H_j/\lambda_j$.

The known junction coefficients change, but no additional or renormalized matrix oracle is synthesized.

For arbitrary positive delays $r_A,r_B,r_K$, the positive action operator in \cref{eq:app-delayed-action} is exactly

\begin{subequations}

\label{eq:app-spectral-action}

\begin{align}

V^\dagger D_{\boldsymbol{r}}V &=\bigl(r_A\lambda_A+r_B\lambda_B\varrho\bigr)I_P +\left(\frac{r_B\lambda_B}{\varrho}+r_K\lambda_K\right)X^\dagger X

\\

&\preceq\left(r_A\lambda_A+2r_B\lambda_B\varrho +r_K\lambda_K\varrho^2\right)I_P.

\end{align}

\end{subequations}

This proves the three weights in \cref{eq:app-spectral-parameters}.

For the inverse bound, $D_{\boldsymbol{r},Q} =(r_B\varrho\lambda_B+r_K\varrho^2\lambda_K)I_Q$, and hence

\begin{align}

\tau_{\boldsymbol{r}} =\left(\frac{r_B\lambda_B}{\varrho}+r_K\lambda_K\right)\|K^{-1}\| \le \frac{r_B\lambda_B}{h_B} +\frac{r_K\lambda_K}{\Delta} \le\max\{\kappa_Br_B,\kappa_Kr_K\}.

\label{eq:app-spectral-tau}

\end{align}

The last step uses $u+v\le2\max\{u,v\}$.

The normalizations satisfy $\lambda_K\ge\|K\|\ge\Delta$ and $\lambda_B\ge h_B$.

Thus all three conditioning parameters are at least one.

Direct substitution verifies the scalar sufficient feedback bound:

\begin{align}

\frac{\lambda_K}{\kappa_K} +\frac{\lambda_B}{\kappa_B\varrho}=\frac\Delta2+\frac\Delta2=\Delta.

\label{eq:app-spectral-kappa-check}

\end{align}

Consequently, \cref{lem:app-cap} provides a common analytic neighborhood.

Its exponent is at most $3|t|(r_A\lambda_A+2r_B\lambda_B\varrho+r_K\lambda_K\varrho^2)$.

The signed-clock construction and the finite Toeplitz approximation in \cref{lem:app-finite-cascade} give

\begin{align}

N=O\!\left[|t|\left(r_A\lambda_A+ \frac{2r_B\lambda_Bh_B}{\Delta} +\frac{r_K\lambda_Kh_B^2}{\Delta^2}\right) +\max\left\{r_A,\frac{2r_B\lambda_B}{h_B}, \frac{2r_K\lambda_K}{\Delta}\right\} \log\frac1\epsilon\right].

\label{eq:app-spectral-horizon}

\end{align}

Apply the integer allocation in \cref{lem:app-allocation} with $a_A=|t|\lambda_A$, $a_B=2|t|\lambda_Bh_B/\Delta$, $a_K=|t|\lambda_Kh_B^2/\Delta^2$, and $b_0=\log(2/\epsilon)$.

Multiplication by $\sum_j C_j/r_j$ and the three executions used for oblivious amplitude amplification proves \cref{eq:app-spectral-cost}.

The counts in this construction are $q_j=3\lfloor(N-1)/r_j\rfloor$, including controlled inactive occurrences.

No preparation of $X|\psi\rangle$ is part of these circuits.

The active-space incident normalization that fixes the finite Cayley length is

\begin{align}

\Lambda_0=\lambda_A+\lambda_Bh_B/\Delta.

\label{eq:app-spectral-Lambda0}

\end{align}

Use this value in \cref{eq:app-J}, and use the resulting $J,N,r_j$ in \cref{eq:app-address}.

In this three-component case, the actual Hilbert space is

\begin{align}

(\mathbb C^N\otimes\mathcal P)\ \oplus\! \bigoplus_{j\in\{A,B,K\}} (\mathbb C^{r_j}\otimes\mathbb C^J\otimes\mathcal L_j),

\label{eq:app-spectral-carrier}

\end{align}

where $\mathcal L_j$ is the full Hilbert space of $\Be{H_j/\lambda_j}$.

Its stage label is a spectator during a component query.

The exact full-space factorization in \cref{eq:app-dyadic} therefore applies without introducing an oracle for the internal response.

With the gate and scratch definitions of \cref{thm:app-gates}, the resulting bounds are

\begin{align}

G_{\rm total} &=O\!\left[\sum_{j\in\{A,B,K\}}q_jG_j+ N(G_{\rm align}+4b)\right],

\label{eq:app-spectral-gates}

\\

n_{\rm total} &=n+O\!\left[a_{\max}+s_{\rm supplied} +\log(2+N+3Jr_*)\right].

\label{eq:app-spectral-space}

\end{align}

Here the $4N$ routing term counts the public stream and three private streams; the remaining query-routing events are at most $3N$ per lift.

Clock preparation uses $O(N)$ elementary gates and is included.

Synthesis and oracle errors use \cref{eq:app-synthesis,eq:app-oracle-error} under the cost model of \cref{def:resource-accounting}.

\end{proof}

For a scalar coupling, the response and energy correction distinguish the physical coupling strength from its oracle normalization: $B=g$, $K=\Delta$, $X=-\overline g/\Delta$, and $BK^{-1}B^\dagger=|g|^2/\Delta$.

With $h_B=|g|$, the internal and coupling action bounds are $\lambda_K|g|^2/\Delta^2 $ and $ 2\lambda_B|g|/\Delta$, respectively.

The latter becomes $2|g|^2/\Delta$ only when the coupling encoding is tight.

The choice $T_Q=\varrho I_Q$ minimizes the displayed time-dependent coupling contribution to the catalyst-weight bound.

It need not minimize time and precision together.

One can retain the same response bound $\|X\|\le\varrho$ and use any internal scale $s>0$.

Here $V_s=\binom{I_P}{X/s}$ is the elimination map.

Form the diagonal action operator $D_{\boldsymbol{r},s}$ from the rescaled normalizations $\lambda_A$, $s\lambda_B$, and $s^2\lambda_K$.

Define $\tau_{\boldsymbol{r},s}=\|D_{\boldsymbol{r},s,Q}^{1/2}(s^2K)^{-1} D_{\boldsymbol{r},s,Q}^{1/2}\|$, with $D_{\boldsymbol{r},s,Q}=QD_{\boldsymbol{r},s}Q$.

The exact calculation above gives

\begin{subequations}

\label{eq:app-spectral-unbalanced}

\begin{align}

V_s^\dagger D_{\boldsymbol{r},s}V_s &\preceq\left[r_A\lambda_A+ r_B\lambda_B\left(s+\frac{\varrho^2}{s}\right) +r_K\lambda_K\varrho^2\right]I_P,

\\

\tau_{\boldsymbol{r},s} &\le\frac{r_B\lambda_B}{s\Delta} +\frac{r_K\lambda_K}{\Delta},

\quad V_s=\begin{pmatrix}I_P

\\

X/s\end{pmatrix}.

\end{align}

\end{subequations}

These follow by multiplying the diagonal support weights exactly as in \cref{eq:app-spectral-action,eq:app-spectral-tau}; the internal scale cancels from the $K$ contribution.

The proof of \cref{lem:app-cap} uses only the action and weighted inverse bounds, so it applies with these expressions.

The same capped allocation gives

\begin{align}

O\!\Bigg[&|t|\left(\sqrt{C_A\lambda_A} +\sqrt{C_B\lambda_B(s+\varrho^2/s)} +\varrho\sqrt{C_K\lambda_K}\right)^2

\\

&+\log\frac2\epsilon\left(C_A+ C_B\max\left\{1,\frac{\lambda_B}{s\Delta}\right\} +\frac{C_K\lambda_K}{\Delta}\right)\Bigg].

\label{eq:app-spectral-unbalanced-cost}

\end{align}

Choose the conditioning parameters as $\kappa_B=2\max\{1,\lambda_B/(s\Delta)\}$ and $\kappa_K=2\lambda_K/\Delta$.

Each contribution to $\tau_{\boldsymbol{r},s}$ is then at most half the maximum delayed conditioning.

Since $s+\varrho^2/s\ge2\varrho$ with equality at $s=\varrho$, this family recovers \cref{eq:app-spectral-cost} and permits a different balance when precision dominates.

This bounds the cost within the stated family of realizations.

\subsubsection{Response bounds for coupled internal sectors}

\label{subsubsec:app-comparison}

Separate spectral bounds also give computable response estimates for an interacting hierarchy.

For internal sectors, write $K_{ik}=Q_iKQ_k$ and $B_i=BQ_i$ for $i,k\ge1$.

Suppose given diagonal gaps and coupling bounds satisfy $\Delta_i>0$, $h_i\ge0$, and $h_{ik}=h_{ki}\ge0$, and

\begin{align}

\|K_{ii}^{-1}\|\le\Delta_i^{-1},

\quad \|B_i\|\le h_i,

\quad \|K_{ik}\|\le h_{ik}

\quad(i\ne k).

\label{eq:app-block-spectral-data}

\end{align}

The diagonal blocks may be indefinite.

Form the real symmetric comparison matrix $M$ with entries

\begin{align}

M_{ii}=\Delta_i,

\quad M_{ik}=-h_{ik}

\quad(i\ne k).

\label{eq:app-comparison-matrix}

\end{align}

These are physical norm bounds.

They may be tighter than sums of the normalizations of the component encodings that implement the blocks.

\cref{prop:app-comparison} replaces a large operator inequality by a sufficient bound from a scalar comparison matrix.

\begin{proposition}[Response bounds from a comparison matrix]

\label{prop:app-comparison}

If $M\succ0$, the internal block $K$ is invertible.

The comparison inverse $M^{-1}$ is entrywise nonnegative, and

\begin{align}

\|K^{-1}\|\le\lambda_{\min}(M)^{-1},

\quad \|Q_iK^{-1}Q_k\|\le(M^{-1})_{ik},

\quad \|Q_iX\|\le\sum_k(M^{-1})_{ik}h_k.

\label{eq:app-comparison-response}

\end{align}

The self-energy also satisfies

\begin{align}

\|BK^{-1}B^\dagger\|\le\sum_{i,k}h_i(M^{-1})_{ik}h_k.

\label{eq:app-comparison-energy}

\end{align}

Consequently, one may take $\Delta=\lambda_{\min}(M)$ and any positive $\varrho_i$ at least the final expression in \cref{eq:app-comparison-response} in the simulation, inverse, and sensitivity results.

Their query factors remain $\lambda_j\chi_j$, with the original component normalizations.

\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}

Write the scalar comparison matrix as a diagonal part minus its nonnegative coupling matrix: $D=\operatorname{diag}(\Delta_i)$, $M=D-H$, $H_{ii}=0$, and $H_{ik}=h_{ik}\ (i\ne k)$.

Congruence by the inverse square root of the positive diagonal matrix gives $D^{-1/2}MD^{-1/2}=I-D^{-1/2}HD^{-1/2}\succ0$.

Consequently, the largest eigenvalue of the rescaled coupling matrix is strictly less than one.

Since this matrix has nonnegative entries, its operator norm equals its largest eigenvalue: for every real vector $\boldsymbol{v}$, the absolute value of its quadratic form is bounded by the quadratic form on the entrywise absolute vector $|\boldsymbol{v}|$.

Thus $\|D^{-1/2}HD^{-1/2}\|<1$, and a Neumann series gives

\begin{align}

M^{-1}=D^{-1/2}\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} (D^{-1/2}HD^{-1/2})^nD^{-1/2}\ge0

\quad\text{entrywise}.

\label{eq:app-comparison-positive-inverse}

\end{align}

To prove invertibility, suppose $K\boldsymbol{v}=0$.

Collect the sector norms in $\boldsymbol{u}=(\|Q_i\boldsymbol{v}\|)_i$.

Multiply each block row by its diagonal-block inverse and take norms.

The triangle inequality gives

\begin{align}

\Delta_i u_i\le\sum_{k\ne i}h_{ik}u_k.

\end{align}

Thus $M\boldsymbol{u}\le0$ entrywise.

Multiplication by the nonnegative matrix $M^{-1}$ implies $\boldsymbol{u}\le0$.

Since $\boldsymbol{u}\ge0$, $\boldsymbol{v}=0$.

In the finite-dimensional oracle spaces, trivial kernel proves invertibility.

For $K\boldsymbol{v}=\boldsymbol{f}$, define $\boldsymbol{f}_{\rm blk}=(\|Q_i\boldsymbol{f}\|)_i$.

The same calculation gives $\Delta_i u_i\le (f_{\rm blk})_i+\sum_{k\ne i}h_{ik}u_k$.

Thus $M\boldsymbol{u}\le\boldsymbol{f}_{\rm blk}$ and $\boldsymbol{u}\le M^{-1}\boldsymbol{f}_{\rm blk}$, so

\begin{align}

\|\boldsymbol{v}\|=\|\boldsymbol{u}\|_2 \le\|M^{-1}\boldsymbol{f}_{\rm blk}\|_2 \le\lambda_{\min}(M)^{-1}\|\boldsymbol{f}\|.

\label{eq:app-comparison-inverse-proof}

\end{align}

For a forcing vector supported on sector $k$, the same inequality gives $\|Q_iK^{-1}\boldsymbol{f}\|\le(M^{-1})_{ik}\|\boldsymbol{f}\|$.

This proves the blockwise inverse estimate.

Set $\boldsymbol{f}=-B^\dagger|\psi\rangle$ for an arbitrary normalized active-space vector.

Then $(f_{\rm blk})_k\le h_k$, and $\|Q_iX|\psi\rangle\|\le\sum_k(M^{-1})_{ik}h_k$.

Taking the supremum proves \cref{eq:app-comparison-response}.

Finally, $BK^{-1}B^\dagger=-\sum_iB_iQ_iX$ has norm at most $\sum_i h_i\|Q_iX\|$.

Substituting the response bounds gives \cref{eq:app-comparison-energy}.

No positivity of $K$ is needed for this norm bound; positivity is used later to infer response bounds from a separately known self-energy bound.

\end{proof}

For two internal sectors with $\Delta_1\Delta_2>h_{12}^2$, the response bounds are explicitly

\begin{align}

\varrho_1=\frac{\Delta_2h_1+h_{12}h_2}{\Delta_1\Delta_2-h_{12}^2},

\quad \varrho_2=\frac{h_{12}h_1+\Delta_1h_2}{\Delta_1\Delta_2-h_{12}^2}.

\label{eq:app-two-sector-response}

\end{align}

This is direct inversion of \cref{eq:app-comparison-matrix}; the positive determinant and diagonal entries ensure positivity.

It includes coupling between the internal sectors in both the numerators and denominator.

Bounds from isolated diagonal inverses alone would omit these terms.

A zero right-hand side in \cref{eq:app-comparison-response} may be replaced by any positive upper bound for use in the junction, or by exact removal of a structurally disconnected sector when that removal is known.

Once the response and gap bounds have been chosen, the scalar conditioning inequality still uses the original component normalizations $\lambda_j$.

For example, assigning every component the common value

\begin{align}

\kappa_j=\max\!\left\{1,\frac1\Delta\max_{i\ge1} \left[\sum_{\ell:\Pi_\ell=Q_i}\lambda_\ell+ \sum_{k\ne i}\sum_{\ell:\Pi_\ell=Q_i+Q_k} \lambda_\ell\frac{\varrho_k}{\varrho_i}\right]\right\}

\label{eq:app-comparison-kappa}

\end{align}

verifies \cref{eq:app-kappa} by division of each row by this common value.

Separate choices can be smaller.

Constructing $M$ and solving its scalar linear system uses the given block bounds, avoiding explicit entries of the exponentially larger internal response.

Obtaining valid block bounds is a separate classical or separately costed computational task.

\subsubsection{Uniform spectral bounds under coarse data}

\label{subsubsec:app-spectral-data}

The response bound used in a cost statement must also apply to the matrix actually encoded.

The following finite perturbation calculation provides this link without a given trial wavefunction.

Perturb the three blocks as $A\mapsto A+\delta A$, $B\mapsto B+\delta B$, and $K\mapsto K+\delta K$, where

\begin{align}

\|\delta A\|\le\lambda_A\eta_A,

\quad \|\delta B\|\le\lambda_B\eta_B,

\quad \|\delta K\|\le\lambda_K\eta_K<\Delta.

\label{eq:app-spectral-data-errors}

\end{align}

For the entire straight interpolation, a uniform response bound is

\begin{align}

\|K(u)^{-1}\|\le\frac1{\Delta-\lambda_K\eta_K},

\quad \|X(u)\|\le \frac{h_B+\lambda_B\eta_B}{\Delta-\lambda_K\eta_K}

\quad(0\le u\le1).

\label{eq:app-spectral-path}

\end{align}

Indeed, the inverse Neumann series converges since $\|K^{-1}u\delta K\|\le\lambda_K\eta_K/\Delta<1$, and $\|B+u\delta B\|\le h_B+\lambda_B\eta_B$.

Multiplication gives \cref{eq:app-spectral-path}.

Let $H_{\mathrm{eff}}'$ be the perturbed Schur complement and put $K'=K+\delta K$.

The resolvent identity and an exact expansion give

\begin{subequations}

\label{eq:app-spectral-perturbation-expansion}

\begin{align}

K'^{-1}-K^{-1}&=-K'^{-1}\delta K K^{-1},

\\

(B+\delta B)K'^{-1}(B+\delta B)^\dagger-BK^{-1}B^\dagger &=\delta B K'^{-1}(B+\delta B)^\dagger +B K'^{-1}\delta B^\dagger +B(K'^{-1}-K^{-1})B^\dagger.

\end{align}

\end{subequations}

Taking norms term by term proves the explicit output bound

\begin{align}

\|H_{\mathrm{eff}}'-H_{\mathrm{eff}}\|\le\lambda_A\eta_A +\frac{2h_B\lambda_B\eta_B+(\lambda_B\eta_B)^2} {\Delta-\lambda_K\eta_K} +\frac{h_B^2\lambda_K\eta_K} {\Delta(\Delta-\lambda_K\eta_K)}.

\label{eq:app-spectral-finite-error}

\end{align}

The first-order coefficients are $\lambda_A$, $2\lambda_Bh_B/\Delta$, and $\lambda_Kh_B^2/\Delta^2$.

The quadratic coupling term and inverse stability denominator quantify the limitation of that first-order interpretation.

Alternatively, \cref{prop:app-data} with the uniform bound in \cref{eq:app-spectral-path} gives a common sensitivity bound for all three perturbations.

Duhamel's identity multiplies either matrix-error bound by $|t|$ for time evolution.

If both active-space matrices are at least $aI$, the inverse resolvent identity multiplies it by $a^{-2}$ for the inverse task.

To compile the coarse model, use valid bounds for that model: the internal singular-value floor is at least $\Delta-\lambda_K\eta_K$ and the coupling norm is at most $h_B+\lambda_B\eta_B$.

When both endpoint oracles have the same normalization $\lambda_B$, this upper bound can be clamped to $\lambda_B$.

The original and perturbed encodings must both obey their stated normalizations; otherwise the common normalization must be updated too.

The cheaper costs $C_j(\eta_j)$ then enter the cost formula with these updated bounds.

For several sectors, \cref{prop:app-comparison} applies uniformly to any comparison matrix formed from lower diagonal bounds and upper coupling bounds valid throughout the data interpolation.

\Needspace{10\baselineskip}

\begin{proposition}[Sensitivity to coarse approximations]

\label{prop:app-data}

Perturb each component while preserving its Hermitian diagonal or coupling-pair support: $\widehat H_j=H_j+\delta H_j$ and $\|\delta H_j\|\le\lambda_j\eta_j$.

Interpolate the component matrices along

\begin{align}

\mathcal H(s)=\sum_j(H_j+s\delta H_j),\qquad0\le s\le1.

\end{align}

Denote its internal block by $K(s)$ and its Schur complement by $H_{\mathrm{eff}}(s)$.

