\section{Introduction}

Quantum impurity models describe a small, strongly interacting fermionic subsystem, called an impurity, coupled to a large noninteracting bath. Historically, Anderson introduced his impurity model to describe localized magnetic moments in metals~\cite{anderson1961localized}. Kondo subsequently used the impurity paradigm to explain the anomalous low-temperature resistance of dilute magnetic alloys~\cite{kondo1964resistance}, and Wilson later gave a nonperturbative solution to the Kondo problem using the numerical renormalization group (NRG)~\cite{wilson1975renormalization}. Impurity models have since found applications ranging from tunneling spectroscopy of single magnetic atoms on metallic surfaces~\cite{madhavan1998tunneling}, to transport through mesoscopic devices such as semiconductor quantum dots and single-molecule transistors~\cite{goldhaber1998kondo,liang2002kondo}.

On the frontier of computational physics and chemistry, the quantum impurity model also plays a central role in embedding methods such as dynamical mean-field theory (DMFT)~\cite{georges1992hubbard,georges1996dynamical,RevModPhys.78.865} and density matrix embedding theory~\cite{knizia2012density}. These methods are widely used to study strong correlation in both lattice models and \emph{ab initio} electronic structure. At a high level, embedding methods handle large interacting systems through a sequence of effective impurity problems, solved iteratively in a self-consistent manner. As such, the success of these heuristics heavily depends on the efficiency and accuracy of the underlying impurity solver. 

Despite the fact that the impurity is localized to a subsystem of small, fixed size, these models are surprisingly nontrivial to simulate. The Kondo problem is a classic example:~a perturbative calculation diverges logarithmically at low temperatures~\cite{kondo1964resistance}. Wilson developed NRG to overcome this breakdown~\cite{wilson1975renormalization}, thereby introducing a nonperturbative method that is still widely used today to solve impurity models at low energies~\cite{bulla2008numerical}. Since then, a diverse toolbox of complementary impurity solvers has emerged, including exact diagonalization, matrix product state (MPS) methods~\cite{PhysRevLett.93.246403,PhysRevB.90.115124}, and continuous-time quantum Monte Carlo (CT-QMC)~\cite{werner2006continuous,gull2011continuous}. More recently, general-purpose many-body methods from quantum chemistry, including configuration interaction~\cite{PhysRevB.86.165128,PhysRevB.100.125165} and coupled-cluster theory~\cite{PhysRevB.100.115154}, have also been adapted as impurity solvers.

While these numerical methods are empirically successful for a wide range of quantum impurity problems, their rigorous efficiency is not well understood. This leads us to the central question:~are impurity models fundamentally easy or hard to simulate? For comparison, the ground-state energy problem for certain Hubbard and electronic-structure Hamiltonians is believed to be intractable even for quantum computers~\cite{schuch2009computational,ogorman2022intractability}. However, the prospects for impurity models are more optimistic:~in seminal work by Bravyi and Gosset~\cite{bravyi2017complexity}, it was shown that the ground-state energy can be estimated to inverse-polynomial error in quasipolynomial time on a classical computer. Building on these ideas, Erakovic \emph{et al.}~\cite{erakovic2025high} later gave polynomial-time quantum algorithms under strong assumptions, such as a constant spectral gap in the bath or access to the ground state's one-body reduced density matrix. Thus, the question of whether there is an unconditional polynomial-time algorithm for this problem, classical or quantum, remained open.

We settle this question by proving that static properties of quantum impurity models are classically easy to compute. Specifically, we give polynomial-time classical algorithms for estimating both the ground-state energy and finite-temperature properties, at any temperature, to inverse-polynomial error. In addition to improving the previous quasipolynomial-time guarantee~\cite{bravyi2017complexity}, this also provides the first rigorous analysis of thermal simulation for impurity models. Our results demonstrate that there is no fundamental obstruction to computing these static properties of impurity models efficiently on classical computers, thereby ruling out any superpolynomial quantum speedup within this regime.

This is particularly relevant to proposals for quantum-enhanced DMFT, which use a quantum computer to solve the underlying impurity problem~\cite{bauer2016hybrid}:~our results imply that a superpolynomial quantum advantage for this application, if any, must come from the dynamical quantities required of the impurity solver. Indeed, we also show as a complementary result that this classical tractability of static properties need not extend to dynamical quantities. We demonstrate this with a canonical example, the nonequilibrium Green's function~\cite{keldysh1965diagram,aoki2014nonequilibrium}, and prove that estimating it to inverse-polynomial error at finite temperature is a complete problem for universal quantum computation. Furthermore, we show that even at infinite temperature, the problem remains complete for the one-clean-qubit model~\cite{knill1998power}, a restricted model of quantum computation that is nevertheless believed to solve problems beyond the reach of classical computers~\cite{shor2008estimating}.

\subsection{Main results}\label{sec:results_summary}

We consider quantum impurity models consisting of a constant-size, arbitrarily interacting impurity coupled to a large free-fermion bath. Let $\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_{2n}$ be the Majorana operators on $n$ fermionic modes. The Hamiltonian takes the form
\begin{equation}
H=H_0+H_{\mathrm{imp}},\qquad
H_0=\alpha \I+\frac{i}{4}\sum_{j,k=1}^{2n}h_{jk}\gamma_j\gamma_k, \label{eq:impurity_model}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}$ is an energy shift and $h$ is a real antisymmetric matrix.
The impurity term $H_{\mathrm{imp}}$ is supported only on the first $m$ modes,
$\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_{2m}$, where $m=\cO(1)$, and is a sum of even-degree products of these operators. The bath term $H_0$ acts extensively but is strictly noninteracting. We make no additional assumptions on the geometry or any spectral properties.

Note that the canonical fermionic ladder operators are simply a linear rewriting of the Majorana operators, $a_j=(\gamma_{2j-1}+i\gamma_{2j})/2$. Under the Jordan--Wigner transformation, Majorana operators are also equivalent to Pauli strings,
$\gamma_{2j-1} = Z_1\cdots Z_{j-1}X_j$ and 
$\gamma_{2j} = Z_1\cdots Z_{j-1}Y_j$. 

\paragraph{Efficient classical simulation of static properties.} Our main result is that both ground- and thermal-state properties of any quantum impurity model can be classically computed in polynomial time. We remark that these runtimes feature exponential dependence in the impurity size $m$, which is constant in our setting. 

\begin{theorem}[Efficient classical simulation, informal (Theorems \ref{thm:classcial_ground_energy} and \ref{thm:classical-thermal})]\label{thm:intro_main_classical} For any quantum impurity model on $n$ fermion modes, and for any $\delta>0$,	 
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Ground states:} There is a classical algorithm with runtime $\poly(n,\delta^{-1})$ to compute the ground energy of the quantum impurity model to additive precision $\delta$.

\item  \emph{Thermal states:} For any inverse temperature $\beta$, there is a classical algorithm with runtime $\poly(n,\beta,\delta^{-1})$ to compute the expectation value of any fermionic Gaussian operator of the form $\exp\l(\sum_{j,k} A_{jk} \gamma_j \gamma_k\r)$ to additive precision $\delta$, or the partition function $Z = \tr(e^{-\beta H})$ to relative precision $\delta$.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

Thus, the above theorem allows efficient classical simulation to arbitrary inverse-polynomial precision. Note that all local fermionic observables are subsumed under the class of fermionic Gaussian operators. For ground states, this improves the previous quasipolynomial-time classical algorithm~\cite{bravyi2017complexity} to polynomial time. It is also worth noting that, although quantum impurity models can be mapped to one-dimensional spin systems, the resulting Hamiltonian may be gapless, so MPS ground-state algorithms for gapped 1D systems do not necessarily apply here~\cite{landau2015polynomial}.

For thermal states, the 1D structure also allows for an MPO representation~\cite{kuwahara2021improved,molnar2014approximating}, but the bond dimension, and hence the computational cost, can grow subexponentially with $\beta$. CT-QMC can also scale exponentially in $\beta$ due to the sign problem~\cite{gull2011continuous,shinaoka2015negative}. In contrast, our result achieves a  polynomial dependence on $\beta$, rigorously extending the regime of efficient simulation to low temperatures.

The main technical ingredient behind both algorithms is a compression lemma for impurity models. After an appropriate change of basis (a Bogoliubov transformation), we show that the ground state, or the relevant part of the thermal state, can be compressed to an exponentially smaller subspace of the full $2^n$-dimensional Hilbert space.
Our approach draws on ideas from numerical methods, notably the logarithmic separation of bath-energy scales and 1D chain transformations pioneered by Wilson for the NRG method~\cite{wilson1975renormalization,PhysRevB.21.1003,bulla2008numerical}.

\begin{lemma}[Compression lemma, informal (Corollaries \ref{cor:ground_compress} and \ref{cor:compression_thermal_extension})]\label{lem:compress_intro}
Consider a quantum impurity model on $n$ fermion modes.
Let $\omega$ denote the spectral gap of the bath term. For any $\delta>0$,
\begin{itemize}
    \item \emph{Ground states:} There exists a ground state whose weight is at least $1-\delta$ on a subspace of dimension $\poly(n,\delta^{-1},\omega^{-1}).$
    \item \emph{Thermal states:} Provided $\beta\omega\ge 9\log n$, the thermal state at inverse temperature $\beta$ has weight at least $1-\delta$ on a subspace of dimension $\poly(n,\beta,\delta^{-1})$.
\end{itemize}
Notably, the dimensions of these subspaces are independent of both the impurity interaction strength $\|H_{\mathrm{imp}}\|$ and the impurity--bath coupling strength.
\end{lemma}

The ground- and thermal-state compression lemmas share the same proof strategy developed in Section~\ref{sec:compress}, with slight differences in the details given in Sections~\ref{sec:compress_ground} and~\ref{sec:compress_thermal}.
The basis for each compressed subspace can be computed in time proportional to its dimension. For ground-energy estimation, the parameter $\omega$ can be artificially adjusted to $\Omega(\delta/n)$ while only changing the ground energy by $\cO(\delta)$. The compression lemma then immediately gives a classical algorithm with desired complexity by diagonalizing the Hamiltonian within the compressed subspace. As a corollary, when $H$ has an inverse-polynomial spectral gap above its ground space, the same techniques also imply efficient preparation of a ground state on a quantum computer \cite{lin2020nearoptimal}.

For thermal states, we only apply the compression lemma to part of the Hamiltonian, leading to the following structural result.

\begin{lemma}[Thermal structural lemma, informal (Section \ref{sec:soft_hard}, Eq.~\eqref{eq:partial_compress})]
Consider any quantum impurity model on $n$ fermion modes. At inverse temperature $\beta$, the thermal state  problem can be efficiently reduced, using the compression lemma, to a Hamiltonian of the form
\begin{equation}
H_{\mathrm{ref}}+V,\qquad \beta\|V\|=\cO(\log n),
\end{equation}
where $H_{\mathrm{ref}}$ is a sum of a compressed interacting Hamiltonian and a free-fermion Hamiltonian, both supported on disjoint sets of modes;~as such, it can be handled efficiently with standard techniques.

Applying CT-QMC to this decomposition gives the classical algorithm of Theorem~\ref{thm:intro_main_classical}, while applying quantum belief propagation \cite{hastings2007quantum} gives a quantum algorithm for preparing the thermal state within trace-distance $\delta > 0$ with gate complexity $\poly(n, \beta, \delta^{-1})$.
\end{lemma}

Here, $H_{\mathrm{ref}}$ is the easy part of the Hamiltonian, while the product of the inverse temperature and perturbation norm, $\beta\|V\|$, controls the simulation cost. Since we show that $\|V\|$ scales as $\beta^{-1}$, this cancels the exponential $\beta$ dependence of both CT-QMC and quantum belief propagation (QBP), resulting in a cost of $\poly(n, \beta, \delta^{-1})$ in both cases.  In contrast, most existing quantum Gibbs samplers for preparing thermal states feature runtime bounds with exponential or worse dependence on $\beta$, even in 1D~\cite{bergamaschi2025fast} or the weakly interacting regime~\cite{tong2025fast,smid2025polynomial}.
For completeness, we provide a rigorous analysis of CT-QMC and QBP in Appendix \ref{sec:CT-QMC} and Appendix \ref{sec:QBP}, respectively. 

\paragraph{Hardness results for out-of-equilibrium simulation.} Our polynomial-time classical algorithms imply that calculating static properties of impurity models do not admit any superpolynomial quantum speedup. As a complementary result, we show that this conclusion does not extend to dynamical properties. To this end, we consider time-dependent impurity Hamiltonians, i.e., of the form \cref{eq:impurity_model} but where the coefficients are now allowed to be efficiently computable functions of time. It was previously shown that such Hamiltonians are universal for quantum computation, in the sense that they can encode any quantum circuit within a logical subspace of the fermions \cite{bravyi2017complexity,brod2014computational}. We extend on this direction to show that this universality persists even in the thermal regime, and over the full fermionic Hilbert space.

Specifically, we consider a canonical object in nonequilibrium physics, the time-dependent Green's function \cite{aoki2014nonequilibrium}. We prove that computing it to small error is complete for one of two central quantum complexity classes, depending on the temperature:~$\BQP$ (bounded-error quantum polynomial time) and $\DQC_1$ (deterministic quantum computation with one clean qubit). The former describes the full power of quantum computation \cite{bernstein1993quantum}, while the latter is a restricted model wherein the quantum computer is only allowed to initialize one pure qubit;~all other qubits are maximally mixed \cite{knill1998power}. The analogy between $\DQC_1$ and impurity models is particularly compelling, as they both feature a small, active subsystem coupled to a large, trivial bath.

\begin{theorem}[Complexity of nonequilibrium Green's functions, informal (Theorems \ref{thm:DQC1-complete} and \ref{thm:BQP-complete})]
    For any $j, k$, consider the dynamical two-point correlation function
    \begin{equation}
        G_\beta(t, t') = \langle \gamma_j(t) \gamma_k(t') \rangle_\beta, \quad \text{where } \gamma_p(s) = U(s)^\dagger \, \gamma_p \, U(s), 
    \end{equation}  
    $U(s)$ is the time evolution under a impurity Hamiltonian $H(s)$ with bounded, time-dependent coefficients, and the thermal expectation is with respect to the Gibbs state of $H(0)$ at inverse temperature $\beta$. For $t, t' = \poly(n)$, estimating $G_\beta(t, t')$ to $1/{\poly(n)}$ precision is:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\DQC_1$-complete for $\beta = 0$, and
        \item $\BQP$-complete for $\Omega(1) \leq \beta \leq \poly(n)$.
    \end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

There are two main technical ingredients behind this theorem. First, the previous universality result for time-dependent impurity models \cite{bravyi2017complexity} relied on an encoding of $q$ logical qubits into $n = \poly(q)$ physical modes \cite{brod2014computational}. However, the Green's function, at $\beta = 0$ for instance, would be $\tr(W)/2^{\poly(q)}$ for some unitary $W$. In contrast, the canonical $\DQC_1$-complete problem is estimating $\tr(C)/2^q$ for any poly-size quantum circuit $C$ on $q$ qubits \cite{shor2008estimating}. Thus this encoding introduces an exponentially small attenuation factor, making it insufficient for proving the desired theorem. To remedy this, we propose a new universal encoding that only uses $n = q + 1$ fermion modes, and therefore only incurs a constant-factor attenuation. The second ingredient concerns proving $\BQP$-hardness even at high temperature, as $\BQP$ is typically understood with respect to pure-state computations. However, we can apply algorithmic cooling~\cite{schulman1999molecular}:~an efficient reversible circuit that concentrates $\cO(q)$ thermal qubits into $q$ almost-pure qubits, with exponentially small error. This turns out to be more than sufficient to encode $\BQP$-complete problems into the finite-temperature Green's function.

\subsection{Technical overview}

We now provide a high-level overview of our classical algorithms, which is the most technically demanding component of this work. Our key technique is a compression lemma showing that the ground state and the relevant part of the thermal state are approximately supported on a small subspace of the full $2^n$-dimensional Hilbert space, as described in Lemma \ref{lem:compress_intro}. For simplicity of the present exposition, we focus attention on the ground state $\ket{\psi}$ here. 

To study this support, recall the annihilation operators
\begin{equation}
a_j\coloneqq \frac{\gamma_{2j-1}+i\gamma_{2j}}{2}
=Z_1\cdots Z_{j-1}\op{0}{1}_j,
\end{equation}
which flips the $j$th fermionic mode from $1$ to $0$ while tracking the parity of all preceding modes. 
The corresponding occupation number is
$n_j\coloneqq a_j^\dagger a_j=\op{1}{1}_j$ whose statistics control the support of $\ket{\psi}$.
For example, if $\bra{\psi}\sum_j n_j\ket{\psi}$ is small, Markov's inequality implies that $\ket{\psi}$ is approximately supported on a small subspace \cite{bravyi2017complexity}.

\paragraph{The Krylov basis.}
A crucial point is that the support of the ground state is dependent on the choice of single-particle basis, and our compression lemma depends critically on this choice. Indeed, for the canonical basis used in previous work~\cite{bravyi2017complexity}, obtained by diagonalizing $H_0$, one can construct an example in which approximating the ground state to constant accuracy requires at least quasipolynomially large support in this basis.\footnote{In this example, $H_{\mathrm{imp}}$ is chosen to be quadratic, so the full Hamiltonian $H=H_0+H_{imp}$ remains free-fermionic and is therefore not hard to solve. Nevertheless, the example shows that although the ground state of $H_0$ has exactly $1$ support in the canonical basis, a constant-size impurity can significantly change the size of the support of the new ground state in the same basis.}
On the other hand, if the one-particle reduced density matrix of the ground state is known, one can instead construct a basis in which the ground state has small support~\cite{bravyi2017complexity,erakovic2025high}. Our first key observation is that a suitable basis can be constructed \emph{without any prior knowledge of the ground state}. We use a bandwise variant of the Krylov basis, whose standard version is commonly used in numerical methods for  impurity models~\cite{wilson1975renormalization} and explored in previous work~\cite{bravyi2017complexity}. Both the Krylov basis and its bandwise variant can be computed efficiently by block tridiagonalizing an $n\times n$ matrix.

The key structural consequence of the Krylov basis is that it transforms every impurity model into a coarse-grained one-dimensional chain of fermions:
\vspace{0.2em}
\input{pics/intro_fig}
\vspace{0.2em}
In this basis, the annihilation operators are grouped into blocks $W_d$:~the first block contains all the impurity modes, each subsequent block contains at most $2m$ bath modes, and the transformed Hamiltonian couples only neighboring blocks. Although a one-dimensional structure alone does not imply efficient classical simulation~\cite{aharonov2009power,gottesman2009quantum}, having the impurity confined to the first block of the chain suggests that the occupation probability $\langle\psi|n_i|\psi\rangle$ may decay exponentially with the distance from the impurity.
This provides the basic intuition for that the ground state has small support in this basis, although the compression lemma requires a much finer analysis of the occupation statistics.

We next illustrate the key proof strategy and the main technical obstacle for the compression lemma through a simple one-dimensional example.

\paragraph{Explicit example and proof strategy.}

To illustrate the proof strategy, we consider a simple impurity model that already has the desired one-dimensional structure. For clarity, in this example we relabel the impurity modes $a_1,a_2$ as $b_1,b_2$, and the bath modes $a_3,\ldots,a_n$ as $a_1,\ldots,a_N$, where $N=n-2$. Consider the Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
H
=
U \left(b_1^\dagger b_1 b_2^\dagger b_2 + b_1^\dagger b_2 +b_2^\dagger b_1\right)
+g\left(
a_1^\dagger b_1
+
b_1^\dagger a_1
\right)
+2\omega\sum_{j=1}^{N}a_j^\dagger a_j
-\frac{\omega}{2}\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}
\left(
a_j^\dagger a_{j+1}
+
a_{j+1}^\dagger a_j
\right)\!\,
\label{eq:overview-example}
\end{equation}
Here $U,g\in \R$ are the impurity interaction strength and  the impurity--bath coupling strength, respectively, and $\omega>0$ is a lower bound on the single-particle energies of the bath. 

Let $\ket{\psi}$ be a ground state. Define $n_j=a_j^\dagger a_j$ for the bath modes $j=1,\ldots,N$. We first illustrate our proof strategy by proving the weaker statement that the occupation probability
$\bra{\psi}n_j\ket{\psi}$ decays exponentially with the distance of the $j$th bath mode from the impurity. Instead of estimating
$\bra{\psi}\sum_j n_j\ket{\psi}$, we consider the one-mode \emph{weighted occupation statistic}
\begin{equation}
F_1
\coloneq
\sum_{j=1}^{N}
e^{j-1}
\bra{\psi}n_j\ket{\psi}.
\end{equation}
Here the subscript $1$ indicates that $F_1$ involves one-mode occupations.

The key tool for estimating $F_1$ is the \emph{commutator identity} with respect to $[a_j,H]$.
The algebraic relations for fermionic ladder operators imply that
$[a_j,H]$ is at most linear in the bath modes. For this particular example,
we have
\begin{equation}
[a_j,H]
=
2\omega a_j
-\frac{\omega}{2}
\left(
a_{j-1}+a_{j+1}
\right)
+
g\delta_{j1}b_1,
\label{eq:overview-commutator-identity}
\end{equation}
where $a_0=a_{N+1}\coloneqq 0$ and  $\delta_{j1}=1$ if $j=1$ and $0$ otherwise. If we let $\alpha_{\mathrm{gs}}$ be the ground energy, then 
$\alpha_{\mathrm{gs}}\I-H \preceq 0$ and hence
\begin{equation}
\bra{\psi}
a_j^\dagger[a_j,H]
\ket{\psi}
=
\alpha_{gs}
\bra{\psi}a_j^\dagger a_j\ket{\psi}
-
\bra{\psi}a_j^\dagger H a_j\ket{\psi}
\leq0.
\label{eq:overview-commutator-bound}
\end{equation}
Inserting the commutator identity into the left-hand side of the above equation leads to a recursion for $F_1$ with a lower-degree contribution, namely a constant $|g|$ that controls the boundary term $\|g\delta_{j1} b_1\|$. Intuitively, this is because the commutator identity in Eq.~\eqref{eq:overview-commutator-identity} never increases the degree in the bath modes:~$[a_j,H]$ remains linear in the bath modes, while the term $g\delta_{j1}b_1$ has strictly lower degree.

To make this intuition precise, let $E$ be the coefficient matrix of the quadratic bath term, which is indexed by the bath modes ${a_j}$ and whose entry $E_{kj}$ is the coefficient of $a_j^\dagger a_k$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:overview-example}.

We also define
$D\coloneqq\sum_{j=1}^N(j-1)\op{j}{j}$,
which records the distance of each bath mode from the impurity.
Define the weighted correlation matrix $M$ and the corresponding weighted bath matrix $B$ by
\[
M_{jk}
\coloneqq
e^{\frac12(j-1)+\frac12(k-1)}
\bra{\psi}a_j^\dagger a_k\ket{\psi},
\qquad
B
\coloneqq
\Reop\l(e^{-D/2}Ee^{D/2}\r).
\]
where $\Reop(A)=(A+A^\dagger)/2$ is the Hermitian part of an operator $A$. Then $M\succeq0$ and $\tr(M)=F_1$. Next, we multiply Eq.~\eqref{eq:overview-commutator-bound} by $e^{j-1}$, insert the commutator identity, take the real part, and sum over $j$. Regrouping the resulting terms using the definitions of $B$ and $M$, we get
\begin{equation}
\tr(BM)
+
g\,\Reop\bra{\psi}a_1^\dagger b_1\ket{\psi}\leq 0.
\label{eq:overview-weighted-commutator}
\end{equation}
The term
$g \Reop\bra{\psi}a_1^\dagger b_1\ket{\psi}$
is bounded by
$
|g\,\bra{\psi}a_1^\dagger b_1\ket{\psi}|
\leq |g|\sqrt{F_1}$
by Cauchy--Schwarz. The key use of the one-dimensional structure is the \emph{positivity of the weighted bath}:~$B\succeq\frac{\omega}{2}\I$. Since $M\succeq 0$, we have
$\tr(BM)\geq\frac{\omega}{2}\tr(M)
=\frac{\omega}{2}F_1.
$
Combining these two bounds with Eq.~\eqref{eq:overview-weighted-commutator}, we obtain
\begin{equation}
\frac{\omega}{2}F_1
\leq
|g|\sqrt{F_1}
\qquad\implies\quad
F_1
\leq
\frac{4g^2}{\omega^2}
\quad \implies\quad \bra{\psi}n_j\ket{\psi}
\leq
\frac{4g^2}{\omega^2}e^{-(j-1)},
\label{eq:overview-F1-recursion}
\end{equation}
where the last equation comes from the definition of $F_1$.

\paragraph{Bandwise Krylov basis and enlarging the impurity.}
Although Eq.~\eqref{eq:overview-F1-recursion} shows exponential decay of the one-mode occupation, a first-moment bound of this form is not sufficient for the compression result in general. The recursion strategy, however, extends to \emph{higher-order} weighted occupation statistics $F_q$, obtained by replacing the one-mode occupation $n_j$ in $F_1$ by the $q$-mode occupation $n_{i_1}\cdots n_{i_q}$. Similar commutator identity and arguments then give a recursion relating $F_q$ to the lower-degree statistic $F_{q-1}$. Controlling this recursion for all $q$ allows us to go beyond the first-moment estimate and obtain a stronger concentration bound.

A second important issue is that the bound in Eq.~\eqref{eq:overview-F1-recursion} depends on $g/\omega$. In general, $\omega$ can be small (for example $\omega \sim 1/n)$ while the impurity--bath coupling can be arbitrary, so $g/\omega$ need not be small. In that case, a bound like Eq.~\eqref{eq:overview-F1-recursion} is not sufficient. To handle this, we use ideas similar to the logarithmic separation of bath-energy scales in NRG~\cite{wilson1975renormalization,PhysRevB.21.1003,bulla2008numerical}. We first perform some preprocessing to ensure $\omega>0$ and set $\omega_\ell=\omega 2^\ell$. We then partition the spectrum of the bath matrix $E$ into intervals $[\omega_\ell,2\omega_\ell)$ for $\ell\geq 0$. On each interval, we restrict $E$ to the corresponding spectral subspace and apply the one-dimensional Krylov-basis transformation to the restricted matrix. We refer to the resulting basis (over the entire single-particle space) as the \emph{bandwise Krylov basis}.

In this way, the single-particle energies in band $\ell$ lie between $\omega_\ell$ and $2\omega_\ell$. At the same time, we enlarge the impurity by including the bath modes in the first Krylov block. The coupling between this enlarged impurity and the remaining bath then comes from the bath matrix $E$ itself, rather than from the original impurity--bath coupling $g$. Since this coupling lies within band $\ell$, its strength is bounded by the energy scale $\cO(\omega_\ell)$ of that band. Thus, the problematic $g/\omega$ coupling is replaced by an effective coupling which is bounded by a constant within each band. 

After this reorganization, the recursion can be applied band by band without requiring the original impurity--bath coupling to be small relative to the bath's spectral gap. We ultimately arrive at our compression lemma that states that the ground state can be compressed into a subspace whose dimension is independent of the impurity interaction strength $U$, the impurity--bath coupling strength $g$, and scales polynomially with $\omega^{-1}$.
For ground-energy estimation to precision $\delta$, we can without loss of generality take $\omega \geq \Omega(\delta/n)$, and hence the resulting compressed subspace has polynomial dimension. More details can be found in Corollary~\ref{cor:ground_compress} and Theorem~\ref{thm:classcial_ground_energy}. A similar compression scheme also works for the relevant part of the thermal state; see Corollary~\ref{cor:compression_thermal_extension} and Theorem~\ref{thm:ctqmc}.

\subsection{Outlook}

In this work, we have established polynomial-time classical algorithms for static properties of quantum impurity models, settling an important open question about their computational complexity~\cite{bravyi2017complexity}, while also providing evidence for possible quantum advantage in certain dynamical problems. The key technical component of our classical algorithms, the compression framework, shows that for impurity models, the ground states and the thermally gapped part of the Gibbs states can be well approximated in low-dimensional subspaces. In particular, our results establish that the ground-state problem for quantum impurity models is fundamentally classically tractable, placing the longstanding empirical success of impurity solvers \cite{wilson1975renormalization,bulla2008numerical,gull2011continuous} on a rigorous footing. They also imply that any superpolynomial quantum advantage in solving impurity models, for example as an application to DMFT~\cite{bauer2016hybrid}, cannot arise from the static problem, but must rather exploit the hardness of simulating dynamics.

An interesting question is whether this compression framework can be extended further, for example excited states, or otherwise adapted to certain dynamical problems \cite{anders2005real,aoki2014nonequilibrium}. Such extensions could broaden the range of impurity properties that admit provably efficient classical simulation. In a separate vein, our current algorithms feature exponential dependence on the impurity size $m$, which we have treated as a fixed constant in this work. Improvements to this dependence could open the possibility of nontrivial algorithms for systems with an extensive number of interacting degrees of freedom, such as the Fermi--Hubbard model \cite{qin2022hubbard}. Finally, while our classical algorithms rule out a superpolynomial quantum advantage for static properties of impurity models, the possibility for a polynomial speedup is left open. One possible route is to identify a substantially smaller subspace that has \emph{constant} overlap with the ground state;~combined with quantum phase estimation, such a property, if it exists, could enable a nontrivial quantum speedup.

\section{Preliminaries}

In this section, we introduce the notation used throughout the paper.



\subsection{Background on fermions}\label{sec:fermi_operator}

We review the fermionic operators, Fock basis, and single-particle basis rotations used throughout the paper. For further background on fermionic quantum information, see~\cite{nielsen2005fermionic,bravyi2005lagrangian}.

\paragraph{Second quantization.}
A system of $n$ fermionic modes is described by $n$  operators $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ satisfying the \textit{canonical anticommutation relations} (CAR)
\begin{align}
    \{a_j,a_k^\dagger\}
    \coloneqq a_j a_k^\dagger+a_k^\dagger a_j
    =\delta_{jk}\I,\qquad
    \{a_j,a_k\} =0,
    \qquad j,k\in[n].
    \label{eq:annihilation-car}
\end{align}
Using the Jordan--Wigner transformation, these operators act on an $n$-qubit space and are represented by
\begin{align}
	a_j = Z_1 \otimes \cdots \otimes Z_{j-1}\otimes \begin{bmatrix}
		0 & 1\\
		0&0
\end{bmatrix},\qquad 1\leq j \leq n, \label{eq:annihilation}
\end{align}
where $Z_k$ represents the Pauli-$Z$ operator on qubit $k$. The operators
$a_j^\dagger$ and $a_j$ are called the \textit{creation and annihilation operators}.

Alternatively, the same fermionic system can be described in terms of $2n$ Majorana operators $\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_{2n}$, defined by
\begin{align}
    \gamma_{2j-1} =a_j+a_j^\dagger,\qquad
    \gamma_{2j} = -i (a_j-a_j^\dagger),\quad\text{where } \{\gamma_j,\gamma_k\}=2\delta_{jk} \I. \label{eq:CAR}
\end{align}
Using the same Jordan--Wigner transformation, these Majorana operators are represented by 
\begin{align}
	\gamma_{2k-1} = Z_1 \otimes \cdots \otimes Z_{k-1} \otimes X_k, \qquad
	\gamma_{2k} = Z_1 \otimes \cdots \otimes Z_{k-1} \otimes Y_k,
\end{align}
where $X_k,Y_k$ are Pauli-$X$, Pauli-$Y$ operators on qubit $k$.

We say that an operator $O$ has \textit{even parity} if it is a sum of even-degree monomials in the creation and
annihilation operators (equivalently, in the Majorana operators), such as $a_1^\dagger a_2$ or $\gamma_1\gamma_2+\gamma_1\gamma_3\gamma_5\gamma_6$. We define \textit{odd parity} similarly. Note that this is distinct from the fermionic parity of states.

\paragraph{Fock basis.}
The annihilation operators $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ define a $2^n$-dimensional Hilbert space called the \textit{fermionic Fock space}. Its standard basis, called the \textit{Fock basis}, is labeled by occupation numbers.
We define
the \textit{vacuum state} $\lvert \mathrm{vac}\rangle$ as the common zero-eigenstate of the operators $\{a_j^\dagger a_j\}_{j=1}^n$. It satisfies
\begin{equation}
    a_j\lvert \mathrm{vac}\rangle=0 \qquad \forall j\in[n].
\end{equation}
For any $x\in\{0,1\}^n$, with respect to the ordering $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ of the annihilation operators, the corresponding \textit{Fock basis state} is defined by
\begin{align}
\lvert x_1,\ldots,x_n\rangle
\coloneqq
(a_1^\dagger)^{x_1}\cdots(a_n^\dagger)^{x_n}
\lvert \mathrm{vac}\rangle.
\end{align}
Here $x_j=1$ means that the $j$th fermionic mode is occupied, while $x_j=0$ means that it is unoccupied. The states
    $\{\lvert x\rangle:x\in\{0,1\}^n\}
$
form an orthonormal basis of the Fock space.

Under the Jordan--Wigner mapping, these Fock basis states correspond directly to the computational basis states $\lvert x_1\cdots x_n\rangle$ of $n$ qubits. In particular, $\lvert \mathrm{vac}\rangle$ corresponds to the all-zero state $\lvert 0^n \rangle$.

\paragraph{Single-particle basis rotation.}

It is convenient to use an $n$-dimensional vector to represent a linear
combination of annihilation operators. For any $\mu\in\C^n$, denote
\begin{align}
    a(\mu)\coloneqq\sum_{j=1}^n \mu_j a_j.
\end{align}
Although $\mu$ is an $n$-dimensional vector, $a(\mu)$ acts on the $2^n$-dimensional Fock space. 
The space $\mathbb{C}^n$ that $\mu$ lives in is the \emph{single-particle space} of the fermions, and any orthonormal basis $\{f_k\}_{k=1}^n$ of $\mathbb{C}^n$ is called a \emph{single-particle basis}.
In particular, the standard basis vector $e_j$ represents the annihilation operator $a_j$, i.e.,
    $a(e_j)=a_j.$
With a slight abuse of terminology, for any subspace $W\subseteq\mathbb{C}^n$, we refer to operators $a(\mu)$ with $\mu\in W$ as fermionic modes in $W$.

A unitary change of basis on the single-particle space induces a corresponding change of fermionic modes. Let $V$ be a unitary on $\C^n$, whose columns $\{Ve_j\}_j$ form a single-particle basis, and define
\begin{equation}
b_j\coloneqq a(Ve_j)=\sum_{k=1}^n V_{kj}a_k,\qquad j\in[n].
\end{equation}
The operators $b_1,\ldots,b_n$ also satisfy the CAR in Eq.~(\ref{eq:annihilation-car}), and hence define a new set of fermionic annihilation operators with a corresponding Fock basis.

These two bases are related by a unitary $\mathcal{U}(V)$ on the full $2^n$-dimensional Fock space called a \textit{single-particle basis rotation}, where  $\mathcal{U}(V)a_j\mathcal{U}(V)^\dagger=b_j$. The unitary $\mathcal{U}(V)$ is fully characterized by the $n\times n$ matrix $V$, and it suffices to work with the new fermionic modes $b_1,\ldots,b_n$. When a qubit representation is needed for algorithmic implementation, one can apply a fermion-to-qubit mapping with respect to these new annihilation operators.

\paragraph{Quadratic Hamiltonians.}
Recall that $e_j\in\mathbb{C}^n$ denotes the $j$th standard basis vector.
For any $n\times n$ Hermitian matrix $P$, we define the corresponding quadratic Hamiltonian by
\begin{align}
\dE(P)\coloneqq\sum_{j,k}\langle e_j,P e_k\rangle a_k^\dagger a_j.
\end{align}
Let $V$ be a unitary and denote its column vectors as $\{f_j\coloneqq Ve_j\}_j$, which form the corresponding single-particle basis. One can check that 
\begin{align}
    \dE(P)\coloneqq\sum_{j,k}\langle e_j,V^\dagger P V e_k\rangle a(f_k)^\dagger a(f_j).\label{eq:V_change_basis}
\end{align}
In particular, when choosing $V$ that diagonalizes $P$, that is    
$P=V\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n)V^\dagger,$ 
and defining new annihilation operators
$b_j\coloneqq a(f_j)$, we have $\dE(P)=\sum_{j=1}^n \lambda_j b_j^\dagger b_j. $
Applying the fermion-to-qubit mapping with respect to the modes $\{b_j\}_{j=1}^n$, we can therefore represent
$\dE(P)=\sum_{j=1}^n \lambda_j \op{1}{1}_j$ and can calculate the full spectrum of $\dE(P)$. Such Hamiltonians are also said to be \textit{free-fermionic}.

The following identities are direct consequences of the canonical anticommutation relations and will be used  in later proofs. For any $\mu,\nu\in\C^n$, one can check that
\begin{align}
    \{a(\mu)^\dagger, a(\nu)\}=\langle \mu,\nu\rangle \I,\qquad \{a(\mu), a(\nu)\}=0,\qquad  [\dE(P),a(\mu)]=-a(P\mu).
\end{align}

\subsection{Preprocessing of the impurity Hamiltonian}\label{sec:hyb_decom}

Consider the quantum impurity model defined in Eq.~(\ref{eq:impurity_model}). 
We denote its ground state as $\rho^{(\infty)}$ and
its thermal state at inverse temperature $\beta$ as 
    $\rho^{(\beta)}\coloneqq e^{-\beta H}/{\tr(e^{-\beta H})}.$

Note that adding a multiple of the identity to the Hamiltonian in Eq.~(\ref{eq:impurity_model}) does not change its ground or thermal states, so we will fix the value of $\alpha\in\R$ later. We assume $\|H\|=\poly(n)$. 

Let $h_{\mathrm{bath}}$ denote the principal submatrix of $h$ corresponding to the bath Majorana operators $\gamma_{2m+1},\ldots,\gamma_{2n}$, and define $\es$ as an upper bound for the bath single-particle energies,  
\begin{align}
\|h_{\mathrm{bath}}\|\leq \es=\poly(n).
\end{align}
Note that $\es$ is independent of the impurity--bath coupling strength.

\paragraph{Preprocessing and the hybridization form.} 
Before turning to the proofs, we perform several preprocessing steps that put the general Hamiltonian into a convenient form.
We regard $\gamma_1,\ldots,\gamma_{2m}$ as the impurity Majorana operators and the remaining Majorana operators as the bath Majorana operators. It is convenient to decompose $H$ into an impurity-only part, a bath-only part, and a hybridization part that couples the impurity and the bath.\footnote{In fact, this is how impurity models are typically presented in the physics literature.} In Appendix~\ref{app:cano_bath}, we show that, by applying a canonical transformation to the bath, one can efficiently construct annihilation operators $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ and write $H$ in the hybridization form:

\begin{equation}
    H
    = K_0+ 
    \dE(E_0)
    +
    \sum_{j=1}^{2m}
    \left(
        a^\dagger(\mu_j)O_j
        +
        O_j^\dagger a(\mu_j)
    \right).\label{eq:impurity_model_hyb}
\end{equation}
Here $a_1,\ldots,a_m$ are called the impurity modes, and the remaining modes are called the bath
modes. The term $K_0$ only acts on impurity modes, $\dE(E_0)$ only acts on the bath modes, and $E_0$ is diagonal. The term $a^\dagger(\mu_j)O_j$ and its Hermitian conjugate describe the hybridization term, where
$a(\mu_j)$ only acts on the bath modes and $O_j$ only acts on the impurity modes and has odd parity. Here the vectors $\{\mu_j\}_j$ need not
be orthogonal.

Define the corresponding \textit{impurity single-particle space} and \textit{bath single-particle space} to be 
\begin{align}
W_{\mathrm{imp}}\coloneqq\operatorname{span}\{e_1,\ldots,e_m\}\subseteq \C^n,\qquad W_{\mathrm{bath}}\coloneqq W_{\mathrm{imp}}^\perp.
\end{align}

In Appendix~\ref{app:cano_bath}, we explain that, when $E_0$ is viewed as a matrix on $W_{\mathrm{bath}}$, without loss of generality for both ground-state and thermal-state simulation, we may assume that $E_0$ is strictly positive; that is, there exists $\omega>0$ such that
\begin{align}
0<\omega\I\leq E_0\leq\es\I. \label{eq:lower_single_particle}
\end{align}



\section{Bandwise Krylov representation}\label{sec:band_krylov_rep}

In the hybridization form Eq.~\eqref{eq:impurity_model_hyb}, we express $H$ in terms of the annihilation operators $\{a_j\}_j$; we write the corresponding single-particle basis as the standard basis $\{e_j\}_j$, i.e., $a_j=a(e_j)$. 

The first key step of our proof is to choose a different basis $\{f_j\}_j$ for the bath single-particle space, which we call the \textit{bandwise Krylov basis}, and re-express the Hamiltonian in terms of the corresponding annihilation operators $\{a(f_j)\}_j\cup \{a(e_j)\}_{j=1}^m$, where the second set corresponds to the basis for the impurity single-particle space. This particular choice of bandwise Krylov basis transforms any impurity model into a one-dimensional structure. 

In this section, we explain how the bandwise Krylov basis $\{f_j\}_j$ is chosen. In Section \ref{sec:1D_from_block_tri}, we explain how a one-dimensional structure can be obtained via block tridiagonalization.  To handle small single-particle energies, we then use the ideas of bandwise Krylov space in Section \ref{sec:band_krylov} and the enlarged impurity  in Section \ref{sec:enlarged_impurity}. To guide the reader, we illustrate and summarize the constructions in these sections in Figure~\ref{fig:1D}.

