\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Combinatorial optimization is a prominent prospective application of quantum computing, yet rigorous quantum advantages remain difficult to establish. Optimization problems arise throughout science and industry, where even modest improvements in solution quality or computational cost can have substantial impact. The central challenge is to determine whether quantum algorithms can offer such improvements over arbitrary classical algorithms~\cite{Abbas_2024,MindTheGaps,VastWorld}.
\emph{Decoded quantum interferometry} (DQI) is a recently proposed quantum algorithmic framework that turns a coherent decoder for the dual of a linear code into a quantum algorithm for optimizing local predicates over the corresponding primal code~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI}. DQI connects these optimization and decoding tasks through a Fourier transform over the underlying finite field, building on the approach pioneered in Regev's quantum reductions~\cite{ATS03,Regev2004,AR2005,Regev2009}. Most prominent is its application to the \emph{optimal polynomial intersection} (OPI) problem, where the primal code is a \emph{Reed--Solomon code} and the dual is a \emph{generalized Reed--Solomon code}. In certain parameter regimes, DQI achieves better approximation guarantees than any \emph{known} polynomial time classical algorithm~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI}. Optimized quantum circuits and concrete resource estimates have further strengthened OPI as a candidate application for demonstrating quantum advantages via DQI~\cite{khattar2025verifiablequantumadvantageoptimized,rosmanis2026nearlylineartimedecodedquantum}.

Whether these approximation guarantees reflect a \emph{genuine quantum advantage}, in the sense of a \emph{provable separation} from \emph{arbitrary} classical algorithms, remains a central open question~\cite{Aaronson2024DQI,MarwahaEtAl2026} and is at the same time a highly contested challenge in the field. We address the following oracle formulation of this question:
\begin{quote}
    \emph{Can the approximation performance of DQI be separated in query complexity from that of arbitrary classical algorithms in a common oracle model?}
\end{quote}

We answer this question affirmatively by constructing a family of \emph{folded OPI instances} based on \emph{folded Reed--Solomon codes}. Each instance consists of a code from this family and a collection of acceptance sets, one per block, each containing exactly half of the block alphabet. These acceptance sets are accessed through separately callable block-membership oracles.
At every fixed rational code rate, this family exhibits a sharp classical threshold: a polynomial-time implementation of Prange's information-set strategy~\cite{Prange1962} reaches this threshold using linearly many membership queries, whereas achieving any fixed improvement with constant success probability requires super-polynomially many \emph{classical} membership queries.

DQI converges to an expected score strictly above the classical threshold at every fixed rational code rate $R\in (0,1)$.
This requires only conventional Reed--Solomon unique decoding based on the Berlekamp--Massey algorithm~\cite{Berlekamp1968,Massey1969}, correcting errors strictly below half the minimum distance of the unfolded dual code.
The score guarantee holds uniformly over all promised instances, and the algorithm uses one coherent query to each block-membership oracle.
Verification and a constant number of repetitions then yield a bounded-error quantum algorithm attaining a score above the classical threshold.
Together with the classical query lower bound, this establishes a query separation for approximate optimization in this oracle model via DQI.
The corresponding question of proving hardness for scalar OPI, based on standard computational assumptions, as studied in Refs.~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI,SunWootters2026,HorinagaYamakawa2026,Jo2026}, remains open.

Our classical query lower bound adapts the exact-search argument of Yamakawa and Zhandry~\cite{YamakawaZhandry2024} to approximation in our balanced membership-oracle model. Their original argument by itself does not rule out a candidate satisfying sufficiently many of the remaining blocks to exceed a certain score. We supplement their framework with a standard conditional binomial-tail estimate, which provides the additional control needed to extend their exact-search query lower bound to approximation.

\Cref{fig:advantage_region} schematically places the present result relative to prior scalar OPI analyses for DQI and beyond~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI,SunWootters2026,HorinagaYamakawa2026,Jo2026} and the Yamakawa--Zhandry exact-search separation~\cite{YamakawaZhandry2024}.

