\subsection{Main result}

We prove the separation on an explicit family of balanced folded-OPI instances. Here, $M$ is the number of block constraints and the growing instance-size parameter, while $R\in(0,1)$ is the fixed target rate of the underlying folded Reed--Solomon code family. For each candidate polynomial, every block tests whether its folded evaluation symbol belongs to an oracle-defined acceptance set, and the score is the fraction of blocks that accept. In our concrete instantiation, every block accepts exactly half of the block alphabet. We focus on this balanced setting because it makes the separation particularly transparent and clean. For the distributional classical statements, the acceptance sets are sampled independently and uniformly from the half-size subsets of the block alphabet.

The two relevant score levels are
\begin{align}
  \alpha_{\rm C}(R)&\coloneq \frac{1+R}{2},
  &
  \alpha_{\rm Q}(R)&\coloneq \frac{1+\sqrt{R(2-R)}}2.
  \label{eq:intro-score-levels}
\end{align}
Prange's information-set strategy~\cite{Prange1962} attains the classical threshold $\alpha_{\rm C}(R)$ using linearly many membership queries in $M$. Exceeding this threshold by any fixed positive amount with constant probability requires super-polynomially many classical queries in $M$. DQI converges to the strictly higher expected score $\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)$~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI}.
Below we give an informal version of \cref{thm:frs-separation}.

\begin{inftheorem}[Balanced DQI separation]
\label{thm:informal-frs-separation}
For every fixed rational rate $R\in(0,1)$, there is an explicit balanced folded-OPI family indexed by block counts $M$, with $M+1$ a power of two, such that for all sufficiently large $M$:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item DQI runs in quantum polynomial time, uses $M$ coherent membership queries, and achieves an expected score $\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)+o(1)$ uniformly over every promised instance.
  \item Prange's information-set algorithm runs in classical polynomial time, uses $\calO(M)$ membership queries, and achieves a score converging in probability to $\alpha_{\rm C}(R)$ under the sampled-oracle distribution.
  \item Any classical algorithm that exceeds $\alpha_{\rm C}(R)$ by a fixed positive amount with constant probability on sampled instances requires super-polynomially many membership queries in $M$, even with unlimited computation between queries.
\end{enumerate}
\end{inftheorem}

Since $\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)>\alpha_{\rm C}(R)$ for every $R\in(0,1)$, verification and a constant number of repetitions of the DQI algorithm give a bounded-error quantum algorithm for every fixed rational threshold $r\in(\alpha_{\rm C}(R),\alpha_{\rm Q}(R))$, using $\calO_{R,r}(M)$ queries on every promised instance. For example, at rate $R=3/10$, $\alpha_{\rm C}(3/10)=0.65$ and $\alpha_{\rm Q}(3/10) \approx0.8571$.
Thus choosing for example $r=0.8$ gives a bounded-error quantum algorithm attaining score at least $0.8$. Since the optimum is at most one, this also gives a $0.8$-approximation.

The block alphabet has size $\exp(\Theta(M\log M))$, with each symbol represented using $\Theta(M\log M)$ bits. The acceptance sets are accessed through membership oracles, and all running-time bounds are measured in the number of blocks $M$.

\paragraph{Beyond DQI.}

Motivated by the beyond-DQI algorithms of Horinaga and Yamakawa~\cite{HorinagaYamakawa2026} and, independently, Jo~\cite{Jo2026}, we ask a secondary question: is the DQI score $\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)$ the best quantum guarantee efficiently attainable in our setting? We discover that it is not: We constructively exceed this score within the same Regev-style framework by replacing \emph{unique decoding} with \emph{complete list decoding} of the folded dual and adapting Jo's coherent fiber-summation method to combine all retained errors with the same syndrome.

For every fixed rational code rate $R\in(0,1)$, define the score envelope
\begin{align}
\alpha_{\rm fib}(R)
&\coloneq
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle \frac12+\sqrt{R(1-R)},&0<R\leq\frac12,\\[1ex]
1,&\frac12<R<1.
\end{cases}
\label{eq:intro-fiber-envelope}
\end{align}
For every fixed rational threshold $0<\beta<\alpha_{\rm fib}(R)$, our construction gives a quantum algorithm running in polynomial time and using linearly many membership queries. For a $1-o(1)$ fraction of sampled instances, this algorithm returns a polynomial with score at least $\beta$ with probability at least $2/3$. These stronger guarantees therefore hold on \emph{typical} sampled instances, whereas the DQI guarantee holds for \emph{every} promised instance.

For fixed $R$ and $\beta$, the complete decoder returns lists of constant size, independent of $M$. This allows all retained errors with the same syndrome to be combined coherently with polynomial overhead. The algorithm and its complexity bounds may depend on the fixed rate and threshold.

For $R>1/2$, we also obtain an \emph{exact-search} guarantee: a separate construction finds a polynomial accepted in every block, with bounded-error success on typical sampled instances and linearly many membership queries. This is stronger than attaining every fixed score threshold below one. The formal statements are given in \cref{thm:sampled-score-hierarchy}.

\Cref{fig:certified_advantage_regimes} summarizes the resulting certified score regimes.

\begin{figure}[!t]
