\subsection{Proof ideas}

The separation in \cref{thm:informal-frs-separation} rests on three ingredients: the DQI score guarantee~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI}, Prange's classical benchmark~\cite{Prange1962}, and a classical query lower bound. The novelty lies primarily in the resulting separation, rather than in a new coding-theoretic or quantum primitive. We do not claim a new code family, decoding paradigm, or DQI mechanism: these ingredients are inherited from earlier work~\cite{YamakawaZhandry2024,Berlekamp1968,Massey1969,JordanEtAl2025DQI}. We establish a common membership-oracle setting in which they yield a rigorous quantum advantage for approximation, together with a classical query lower bound identifying the sharp classical threshold. This requires verifying the folded DQI score guarantee and extending the Yamakawa--Zhandry exact-search query lower bound to approximation using a standard conditional binomial-tail estimate.

\paragraph{DQI on folded codes.}
DQI turns a coherent decoder for the dual code into an optimization algorithm over the primal code~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI}. Building on the folded construction of Jordan et al., we verify that the semicircle score guarantee extends to balanced folded predicates under membership-oracle access. For our folded Reed--Solomon family, the required decoder operates strictly below half the unfolded dual code's minimum distance, so conventional Reed--Solomon unique decoding suffices~\cite{Berlekamp1968,Massey1969}. This yields the claimed DQI score uniformly over every promised instance.

\paragraph{Prange's algorithm on folded codes.}
A block version of Prange's algorithm~\cite{Prange1962} constructs a solution by selecting an accepted symbol in each of a suitable number of blocks and interpolating a low-degree polynomial through their evaluations. These blocks are satisfied by construction. The remaining acceptance sets are independent of the interpolated polynomial, so each remaining block accepts with probability one half. This yields the classical score claimed above.

\paragraph{From exact search to approximation hardness.} 
Our classical query lower bound adapts the classical hardness proof of Yamakawa and Zhandry~\cite{YamakawaZhandry2024}. Their \emph{exact-search argument} exploits the requirement that a successful candidate must be accepted on every still-unqueried block. 
For \emph{approximation}, we instead need to control the probability that sufficiently many of these blocks accept.
We therefore augment their proof with a standard probabilistic estimate. List recoverability bounds the number of candidate codewords for which, after a polynomial number of queries, membership is known on a sufficiently large fraction of blocks. Then, a conditional binomial-tail bound makes it exponentially unlikely that any one of these candidates is accepted on sufficiently many of its unqueried blocks, thus leading to a score above a certain threshold. A union bound over the list-recovery candidates then rules out every constant improvement over Prange's algorithm within polynomially many queries.

\subsection{Related work}

\paragraph{Provable super-polynomial quantum advantages in optimization.}

Conditional quantum advantages for approximate optimization are known in the standard model. Assuming the classical hardness of RSA inversion, Pirnay et al.\ use cryptographic and computational-learning reductions to obtain a super-polynomial quantum advantage for approximating specially constructed integer-programming instances~\cite{Pirnay2024}. Their quantum algorithm extracts 
the public RSA parameters, uses Shor's algorithm~\cite{Shor1994} to reconstruct the secret key, and converts it into a solution with the claimed approximation guarantee. Szegedy argues that the PCP theorem and Shor's algorithm yield analogous conditional approximation separations~\cite{szegedy2022}, even though this path is yet to be formulated in full. These routes transport a Shor-based speedup through classical reductions, whereas the present separation arises directly from coherent decoding and Fourier interference.
In an oracle model, Leng et al.\ establish unconditional super-polynomial quantum--classical separations for discrete and continuous optimization tasks using adiabatic quantum optimization and quantum Hamiltonian descent, respectively~\cite{leng2025subexponentialquantumspeedupoptimization}.

\paragraph{The power and limitations of DQI and related Regev-style algorithms.}
The Fourier--decoding mechanism of DQI~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI} belongs to the broader lineage of Regev-style quantum reductions~\cite{ATS03,Regev2004,AR2005,Regev2009}. Garrido and Chailloux study whether it can yield a non-oracular advantage for Reed--Solomon decoding problems related to discrete logarithms, although the available efficient decoders do not reach the required parameter regime~\cite{garrido2026}. Yamakawa and Zhandry establish a quantum--classical random-oracle separation for exact search on folded Reed--Solomon instances~\cite{YamakawaZhandry2024}. Briaud et al.\ give an expected polynomial-time quantum algorithm for exact search in succinct random low-degree multivariate polynomial systems~\cite{BriaudEtAl2025Multivariate}; for degrees greater than two, the corresponding classical hardness remains conjectural.

Jordan et al.~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI} discuss the connection between DQI and Yamakawa--Zhandry~\cite{YamakawaZhandry2024} and develop a folded-DQI construction with an oracle implementation in their Supplementary Section~11. However, they do not explicitly establish the corresponding semicircle score guarantee for folded codes. Building on this construction, we verify that guarantee within the strict distance regime and extend the Yamakawa--Zhandry classical hardness argument from exact search to approximation. \Cref{sec:yz-dictionary} further clarifies this connection and compares the two oracle models.

Scores above the DQI semicircle value are known for OPI, both existentially and through efficient quantum algorithms. Sun and Wootters prove an existential improvement for balanced OPI over prime fields~\cite{SunWootters2026}. Horinaga and Yamakawa and, independently, Jo subsequently give constructive worst-case quantum algorithms beyond the DQI regime ~\cite{HorinagaYamakawa2026,Jo2026}. Although their constructions differ, both go beyond unique decoding by using a list decoder for the dual code. Following this line, our stronger quantum results replace the unique decoder by a complete list decoder for the folded dual and adapt Jo's coherent fiber-summation method to combine all returned labels with the same syndrome.

A related line of work replaces unique decoding or list decoding by \emph{soft decoding}. Chailloux and Tillich develop Regev-style quantum reductions based on soft information from a decoder ~\cite{chailloux2024softdecoders}, and Chailloux subsequently connects this framework directly to scalar OPI in an average-case setting ~\cite{chailloux2025opixsoftdecoders}.
Other recent developments include optimization using locally quantum decoders~\cite{shutty2026optimizationusinglocallyquantumdecoders}, extensions using algebraic geometry codes~\cite{GuJordan2025HOPI,matthews2026cofidqicurvebasedoptimalfunction}, and multivariate extensions of DQI~\cite{bu2026multivariatedecodedquantuminterferometry}. Hamiltonian DQI extends the approach to Gibbs-state preparation and Hamiltonian optimization~\cite{schmidhuber2025hamiltoniandecodedquantuminterferometry,bu2026hamiltoniandecodedquantuminterferometry}. Shang and Stilck Fran\c{c}a develop a common relative-decoding framework encompassing DQI and its Hamiltonian extension~\cite{ShangFranca2026}.

Several results delimit where a DQI advantage can occur. PCP-based inapproximability establishes strong $\mathsf{NP}$-hardness thresholds for general and bounded-degree max-LINSAT~\cite{KramerSchubertEisert2026Tight,KramerSchubertEisert2026Bounded}. These thresholds constrain quantum as well as classical polynomial-time algorithms under the corresponding complexity assumptions, but do not preclude an advantage on structured families. Marwaha et al.\ show that binary DQI constructively realizes Tietavainen's covering-radius bound~\cite{Tietavainen1990} and that DQI's output distributions can be approximately sampled in randomized polynomial time with an $\mathsf{NP}$ oracle~\cite{MarwahaEtAl2026}. Parekh rules out an asymptotic DQI advantage for the relevant MaxCut families~\cite{parekh2025DQI_maxcut}, while Anschuetz, Gamarnik, and Lu derive obstructions for random local max-$k$-XORSAT under their stated ensemble and decoder hypotheses~\cite{anschuetz2025DQI}. On related random-regular instances, structure-aware classical heuristics can match the locally quantum decoder studied by Shutty et al.~\cite{shutty2026optimizationusinglocallyquantumdecoders}. Classical Markov-chain Monte Carlo experiments also reproduce DQI approximation ratios at substantial finite sizes, with data consistent with exponential scaling on the tested OPI instances~\cite{gilfuster2026mcmc}. These works provide important evidence about the power and limitations of DQI.

\subsection{Organization}

After introducing notation and the general problem definitions in \cref{sec:preliminaries}, we define the balanced folded Reed--Solomon family and state the concrete separation in \cref{sec:concrete-problem}. We prove the separation in \cref{sec:concrete-proof}, and show in \cref{sec:sampled-score-hierarchy} that stronger quantum algorithmic guarantees exist. We conclude in \cref{sec:discussion}.

The appendices provide the technical foundations supporting the main results. \Cref{app:dqi} gives the complete score analysis and implementation of DQI. \Cref{app:ordinary-dqi-consequences} proves that sampled instances typically admit perfect solutions and uses this to transfer the classical query lower bound for achieving any fixed score above $\alpha_{\rm C}(R)$ to approximation ratios, completing the DQI ratio separation. \Cref{sec:stronger-sampled-algorithms} proves the stronger guarantees on typical sampled oracles, including exact search, and relates these constructions to the Yamakawa--Zhandry setting.

\section{Preliminaries}
\label{sec:preliminaries}

This section fixes notation, reviews linear and folded codes, and gives a folded code formulation of the optimization problem and access conventions used below.

\subsection{Notation}
Throughout, let $q$ be a prime power and let $\F_q$ be the field of order $q$ with multiplicative group $\F_q^\times\coloneq\F_q\setminus\{0\}$. For a positive integer $L$, write $[L]\coloneq\{0,\ldots,L-1\}$. We write $\mathbf 0$ and $\mathbf 1$ for the all-zero and all-one vectors, respectively, with their dimensions determined by context. For any statement $E$, let $\mathds 1_{E}$ denote its indicator function.

\subsection{Codes and folding}
\label{sec:codes-and-folding}
We recall standard coding-theoretic definitions and facts; see, e.g., Ref.~\cite{Lindell2010CodingTheory} for background on linear and Reed--Solomon codes. An $[N,K]_q$ linear code is a $K$-dimensional $\F_q$-linear subspace $C\subseteq\F_q^N$. The rate of an $[N,K]_q$ code is $K/N$. Equivalently, if $B\in\F_q^{N\times K}$ has full column rank, then
\begin{align}
 C &= \operatorname{im}B = \{Bx:x\in\F_q^K\}
\end{align}
is an $[N,K]_q$ code. With respect to the standard inner product on $\F_q^N$, its dual code is
\begin{align}
 C^\perp &\coloneq{} \{y\in\F_q^N:y^Tc=0\text{ for every }c\in C\}
 = \ker B^T.
 \label{eq:prelim-dual-code}
\end{align}

\begin{definition}[Folding]
\label{def:folding}
Choose $N$ such that $N=hM$ with $h,M \in \mathbb{N}$. For a word $c=(c_t)_{t\in[N]}\in\F_q^N$, define its $h$-folding by
\begin{align}
 \operatorname{Fold}_h(c)
 &\coloneq
 \left(
  (c_{ih+j})_{j\in[h]}
 \right)_{i\in[M]}
 \in
 (\F_q^h)^M
 \label{eq:folding-map} \\
 &= \left[ (c_0, \dots, c_{h-1}), \dots, (c_{N-h}, \dots, c_{N-1})\right].
\nonumber
\end{align}
For a linear code $C\subseteq\F_q^N$, its $h$-folded version is
\begin{align}
 C_{(h)}
 &\coloneq
 \operatorname{Fold}_h(C)
 =
 \{\operatorname{Fold}_h(c):c\in C\}
 \subseteq
 (\F_q^h)^M.
 \label{eq:folded-code}
\end{align}

When $h=1$, we call the code \emph{scalar}, and use this term for related concepts such as the distance or decoder defined at block size one. Explicitly, $C_{(1)}=C$. Since $\operatorname{Fold}_h$ only regroups coordinates into blocks, $C_{(h)}$ contains the same data as $C$, and in particular has the same dimension and rate.
\end{definition}

We next define suitable notions of block support and block weight for any block word $y=(y_i)_{i\in[M]}\in(\F_q^h)^M$:
\begin{align}
 \operatorname{supp}_{\rm blk}(y)
 &\coloneq{}
 \{i\in[M]:y_i\neq \mathbf{0}\},
 &
 \operatorname{wt}_{\rm blk}(y)
 &\coloneq{}
 \abs{\operatorname{supp}_{\rm blk}(y)}.
 \label{eq:prelim-block-support-weight}
\end{align}
We further introduce the following notion of block distance and block inner pai for any pair of block words $y,y'\in(\F_q^h)^M$:
\begin{align}
 d_{\rm blk}(y,y')
 &\coloneq\operatorname{wt}_{\rm blk}(y-y')
 =\abs{\{i\in[M]:y_i\ne y'_i\}}.
 \label{eq:prelim-block-word-distance}\\
 \langle y,y'\rangle_{\rm blk}
 &\coloneq{}
 \sum_{i\in[M]}y_i^Ty_i'.
 \label{eq:folded-block-pairing}
\end{align}
We note that the block inner product corresponds to the regular inner product after unfolding.

For a linear block code $C_{(h)}\subseteq(\F_q^h)^M$, its minimum block distance is
\begin{align}
 d_{\rm blk}(C_{(h)})
 &\coloneq{}
 \min_{\mathbf{0}\neq y\in C_{(h)}}\operatorname{wt}_{\rm blk}(y),
 \label{eq:prelim-block-distance}
\end{align}
with the convention $d_{\rm blk}(\{0\})=+\infty$. Again we highlight that we use the term ``scalar'' to refer to the $h=1$ case, and this definition recovers the usual notion of distance~\cite{Lindell2010CodingTheory}.

Throughout, we use \emph{unique decoding} to mean bounded-distance decoding within a prescribed radius strictly below half the relevant minimum distance. Error weights and distances are measured in scalar coordinates or folded blocks, as specified.

In the problems below, one whole $h$-tuple---rather than each scalar coordinate separately---is the unit on which acceptance is counted. Folding only regroups the coordinates, the field $\mathbb{F}_q$ we work over and the code rate $K/(hM)=K/N$ are preserved.

We write $\F_q[Y]$ for the set of polynomials in the variable $Y$ with coefficients in $\F_q$. Thus every $Q\in\F_q[Y]$ with $\deg Q<K$ can be written uniquely as
\begin{align} 
Q(Y) &= \sum_{r\in[K]}x_rY^r, & x_r&\in\F_q.
\end{align}

\begin{definition}[Generalized Reed--Solomon code]
\label{def:grs-evaluation-code}
Let $\boldsymbol\alpha=(\alpha_t)_{t\in[N]}$ consist of pairwise distinct elements of $\F_q$, and let $\boldsymbol v=(v_t)_{t\in[N]}\in(\F_q^\times)^N$ be nonzero coordinate multipliers. For $0\leq K\leq N\leq q$, the corresponding generalized Reed--Solomon code is
\begin{align}
\operatorname{GRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,\boldsymbol v,K}
&\coloneq
\left\{
 \bigl(v_tQ(\alpha_t)\bigr)_{t\in[N]}:
 Q\in\F_q[Y],\ \deg Q<K
\right\}
\subseteq\F_q^N,
\label{eq:grs-evaluation-code}
\end{align}
with the convention $\operatorname{GRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,\boldsymbol v,0}\coloneq\{0\}$. The ordinary \emph{Reed--Solomon code} $\operatorname{RS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K}$ is the special case $\boldsymbol v=\boldsymbol 1$.
\end{definition}

Because the evaluation points are distinct and $N\geq K$, evaluation is injective on polynomials of degree less than $K$. Hence $\operatorname{GRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,\boldsymbol v,K}$ is an $[N,K]_q$ linear code, and in particular so is $\operatorname{RS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K}$. The generalized form is needed because the dual of an ordinary Reed--Solomon code generally has nontrivial coordinate multipliers, as we will see below.

With respect to the standard inner product on $\F_q^N$, the dual of a Reed--Solomon code is a generalized Reed--Solomon code on the same evaluation points
\begin{align}
\bigl(\operatorname{RS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K}\bigr)^\perp
&=
\operatorname{GRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,\boldsymbol\nu,N-K}.
\label{eq:rs-dual-grs}
\end{align}
For background on this duality, see Ref.~\cite[Section~6]{Lindell2010CodingTheory} and Ref.~\cite[Section~4.3.1]{YamakawaZhandry2024}. For arbitrary pairwise distinct evaluation points, the dual multipliers may be chosen as
\begin{align}
\nu_t
&\coloneq
\left(
\prod_{\substack{s\in[N]\\s\neq t}}
(\alpha_t-\alpha_s)
\right)^{-1},
&
\boldsymbol\nu
&\coloneq(\nu_t)_{t\in[N]}.
\label{eq:rs-dual-multipliers}
\end{align}
The distinctness of the evaluation points makes every multiplier nonzero, including when one evaluation point is zero.

This dual has dimension $N-K$ and, when $K<N$, scalar minimum distance $K+1$. Since all $\nu_t$ are nonzero, coordinatewise division by $\nu_t$ maps it to $\operatorname{RS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,N-K}$ and preserves error support. Thus decoding the generalized Reed--Solomon dual reduces to ordinary Reed--Solomon decoding after a known coordinatewise rescaling; the decoded word is then mapped back by restoring the multipliers.

For the full nonzero evaluation set used below, $N=q-1$ and $\alpha_t=\gamma^t$ for a primitive $\gamma\in\F_q^\times$ and $t\in[N]$. These points enumerate $\F_q^\times$. In characteristic two, the multipliers therefore simplify to
\begin{align}
\nu_t&=\alpha_t.
\label{eq:dqi-full-length-dual-multipliers}
\end{align}
Indeed, differentiating $\prod_{s\in[N]}(Y-\alpha_s)=Y^{q-1}-1$ at $Y=\alpha_t$ gives $\prod_{s\neq t}(\alpha_t-\alpha_s)=\alpha_t^{-1}$.

\begin{definition}[Folded Reed--Solomon code]
\label{def:folded-rs-code}
Let $h,M$ be positive integers, put $N=hM$, and assume $0<K\leq N\leq q$. Let $\boldsymbol\alpha=(\alpha_t)_{t\in[N]}$ consist of pairwise distinct elements of $\F_q$. The $h$-folded Reed--Solomon code is
\begin{align}
\operatorname{FRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K,h}
\coloneq
\operatorname{Fold}_h
\bigl(\operatorname{RS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K}\bigr)
=
\left\{
 \left(
  \bigl(Q(\alpha_{ih+j})\bigr)_{j\in[h]}
 \right)_{i\in[M]}:
 Q\in\F_q[Y],\ \deg Q<K
\right\}
\subseteq(\F_q^h)^M.
\label{eq:folded-rs-code}
\end{align}
It is an $\F_q$-linear code of dimension $K$.
\end{definition}

In this work, we only consider folded Reed--Solomon codes with $N=hM\leq q-1$ whose evaluation points are consecutive powers of a primitive element $\gamma\in\F_q^\times$, namely $\alpha_{ih+j}\coloneqq\gamma^{ih+j}$ for $i\in[M]$ and $j\in[h]$.

Under our definition for block inner product, folding commutes with taking duals~\cite[Section~4.1]{YamakawaZhandry2024}. Combining this observation with \eqref{eq:rs-dual-grs} gives
\begin{align}
\bigl(\operatorname{FRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K,h}\bigr)^\perp
&=
\operatorname{Fold}_h
\bigl(
 \operatorname{GRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,\boldsymbol\nu,N-K}
\bigr).
\label{eq:folded-rs-dual-grs}
\end{align}
The coordinatewise rescaling above also preserves block error support.

\subsection{Optimization problems and oracle access}
\label{sec:optimization-problems}

We formulate the objective once over a block-linear code. Folded max-LINSAT permits an arbitrary linear encoder, i.e., a matrix $B$, while folded \emph{optimal polynomial intersection} (OPI) specializes the encoder to low-degree polynomial evaluation.

\paragraph{Folded max-LINSAT.} 

We start by defining folded max-LINSAT over $\F_q$.

\begin{definition}[Folded max-LINSAT over $\F_q$]
\label{def:folded-max-linsat}
Fix integers $h,M,K>0$, put $N\coloneq hM$, assume $0<K<N$, and let $\Sigma\coloneq\F_q^h$. An instance consists of a full-column-rank matrix
\begin{align}
 B
 &\coloneq
 \begin{bmatrix}
 B_0\\
 \vdots\\
 B_{M-1}
 \end{bmatrix}
 \in\F_q^{N\times K},
 &
 B_i
 &\in\F_q^{h\times K},
 \label{eq:folded-max-linsat-matrix}
\end{align}
and acceptance sets $A_i\subseteq\Sigma$ for $i\in[M]$. Its feasible block words form the code
\begin{align}
 C
 &\coloneq
 \left\{
 (B_ix)_{i\in[M]}:
 x\in\F_q^K
 \right\}
 =
 \operatorname{Fold}_h(\operatorname{im}B)
 \subseteq\Sigma^M.
 \label{eq:max-linsat-feasible-code}
\end{align}
Because $B$ has full column rank, every feasible word $c \in C$ has a unique message $x\in\F_q^K$. For $c=(c_i)_{i\in[M]}\in C$, define
\begin{align}
 \scor(c)
 &\coloneq
 \frac1M\sum_{i\in[M]}\mathds{1}_{\{c_i\in A_i\}}.
 \label{eq:folded-max-linsat-score}
\end{align}
The optimization task is to find $c\in C$, equivalently $x\in\F_q^K$, maximizing this score. For a threshold $\beta\in[0,1]$, the threshold-search task asks for a feasible $c$ satisfying $M\scor(c)\geq\ceil{\beta M}$. If no such word exists, the relation has no valid output.

For a fixed instance, define $\operatorname{OPT}\coloneq\max_{c\in C}\scor(c)$. The multiplicative approximation ratio of a feasible word is
\begin{align}
 \operatorname{ratio}(c)
 &\coloneq
 \begin{cases}
 \displaystyle
 \frac{\scor(c)}
   {\operatorname{OPT}},
 &\operatorname{OPT}>0,\\[2ex]
 1,
 &\operatorname{OPT}=0.
 \end{cases}
 \label{eq:general-ratio-definition}
\end{align}
Thus $c$ is an $r$-approximation exactly when $\scor(c)\geq r\operatorname{OPT}$.
\end{definition}

We assume without loss of generality that the encoder has full column rank, since deleting redundant columns preserves its image. When $h=1$, this is scalar max-LINSAT over $\F_q$.

\paragraph{Folded OPI.}
\emph{Optimal polynomial intersection} (OPI) asks for a low-degree polynomial whose evaluations belong to specified acceptance sets at as many evaluation points as possible. We use \emph{scalar OPI} to refer to the formulation studied in Refs.~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI,SunWootters2026,HorinagaYamakawa2026,Jo2026}, where each acceptance test concerns a single polynomial evaluation in $\F_q$. In \emph{folded OPI}, each test concerns a tuple of $h$ evaluations of the same polynomial, tested jointly against a specified acceptance set in $\F_q^h$.

Under the hypotheses of \cref{def:folded-max-linsat}, additionally assume $N=hM\leq q$ and choose pairwise distinct evaluation points $\boldsymbol\alpha=(\alpha_t)_{t\in[N]}$. Represent a polynomial of degree less than $K$ by its coefficient vector:
\begin{align}
Q(Y) &\coloneq \sum_{r\in[K]}x_rY^r, &
x &\coloneq (x_r)_{r\in[K]} \in\F_q^K.
\end{align}
Polynomial evaluation is linear in the coefficients: multiplying the row $(1,\alpha,\ldots,\alpha^{K-1})$ by the coefficient vector $x$ gives $Q(\alpha)$. Write $\alpha_{i,j}\coloneq\alpha_{ih+j}$ for $i\in[M]$ and $j\in[h]$. For block $i$, we collect the $h$ evaluation rows into $B_i$; stacking these matrices gives the full encoder:
\begin{align}
B_i &\coloneq
\begin{bmatrix}
1&\alpha_{i,0}&\cdots&\alpha_{i,0}^{K-1}\\
\vdots&\vdots&&\vdots\\
1&\alpha_{i,h-1}&\cdots&\alpha_{i,h-1}^{K-1}
\end{bmatrix}
\in\F_q^{h\times K}, \qquad i\in[M], \\
B &\coloneq
\begin{bmatrix}
B_0\\
\vdots\\
B_{M-1}
\end{bmatrix}
\in\F_q^{hM\times K}.
\end{align}
Thus $B_i x=c_i(Q)$ is the evaluation block of $Q$ at the $i$th group of points, and $Bx$ collects all $M$ blocks. Since the evaluation points are distinct and $N\geq K$, this encoder has full column rank. Thus the coefficient vector, the polynomial, and its folded evaluation word $c(Q)\coloneq(c_i(Q))_{i\in[M]}$ are equivalent descriptions of a feasible solution. The feasible code is precisely $\operatorname{FRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K,h}$ from \cref{def:folded-rs-code}.

Given acceptance sets $A_i\subseteq\F_q^h$, the objective is to find a polynomial $Q\in\F_q[Y]$ of degree less than $K$ that maximizes the accepted-block fraction $\scor(Q)\coloneq\frac1M\sum_{i\in[M]} \mathds{1}_{\{c_i(Q)\in A_i\}}$. We refer to this specialization of folded max-LINSAT as \emph{folded OPI}. Each accepted block contributes one unit to the unnormalized objective. The acceptance sets may impose arbitrary joint conditions on the $h$ evaluations within a block; they need not be Cartesian products of scalar acceptance sets. Folding therefore preserves the polynomial space while allowing a different objective and unit of oracle access: one membership query tests one complete block value.

For $h=1$, one has $M=N$ and recovers scalar OPI. The folded OPI formulation above fits the extension of DQI to block predicates developed in Ref.~\cite{JordanEtAl2025DQI}.

The score and approximation-ratio conventions are inherited from \cref{def:folded-max-linsat}, with
\begin{align}
\scor(Q) &\coloneq \scor(c(Q)),
& \operatorname{ratio}(Q) &\coloneq \operatorname{ratio}(c(Q)).
\end{align}

\subsection{Acceptance sets and membership-oracle access}
\label{sec:balanced-oracle}

The encoder $B$ and the block alphabet $\Sigma$ are specified explicitly and independently of the oracle. The oracle-dependent part of an instance is the collection of acceptance sets $\mathcal A=(A_i)_{i\in[M]}$. For each block $i\in[M]$, define its Boolean membership function by
\begin{align}
O_{A_i}(z)
&\coloneq \mathds{1}_{\{z\in A_i\}},
\qquad z\in\Sigma.
\label{eq:membership-table}
\end{align}
The instance provides the family of membership oracles
\begin{align}
\calO_{A_i}\ket{z,t}
&\coloneq
\ket{z,t\oplus O_{A_i}(z)},
\qquad i\in[M],\quad t\in\F_2.
\label{eq:general-membership-oracle}
\end{align} 
Throughout, one query means one invocation of one fixed oracle $\mathcal O_{A_i}$, with $i$ chosen classically. The symbol and answer registers may be in arbitrary superposition, but the interface provides neither a coherent block-index register nor a multiplexed oracle at unit cost. Thus one query tests whether one complete block symbol $z$ is accepted in one chosen block.

When $|\Sigma|$ is even, we call the oracle family \emph{balanced} if $|A_i| =|\Sigma|/2$ for every $i \in [M]$.
The preceding optimization definitions also apply when the acceptance sets are given explicitly; the separation below uses only the membership interface above.

\section{Statement of the concrete separation}
\label{sec:concrete-problem}

We first define the explicit balanced folded-OPI instance family and then state the concrete separation theorem for DQI.

\subsection{The instance family and oracle model}
\label{sec:density-oracle-model}

For the folded Reed-Solomon code, fix a rational target rate $R\in(0,1)$. For a positive integer $M$ such that $M+1$ is a power of two, set
\begin{align}
     h&\coloneq M+2,
     &
     N&\coloneq hM,
     &
     q&\coloneq N+1=(M+1)^2,
     &
     K&\coloneq \floor{RN}.
     \label{eq:frs-parameters}
\end{align}
We also write
\begin{align}
    J &\coloneq \floor{K/h} = \floor{RM}.
\label{eq:frs-complete-block-count}
\end{align}
We can choose an arbitrary target symbol in each of $J$ blocks and interpolate a polynomial of degree less than $K$ matching all these targets simultaneously. Each target specifies $h$ evaluation values, so $J$ is the largest number of complete blocks for which this is always possible: $hJ\leq K<h(J+1)$. Prange's algorithm in \cref{sec:proof-prange} uses this freedom to make the selected blocks accept.
We call $M$ \emph{admissible for $R$} when, in addition, $K\geq h$. For every fixed $R>0$, this excludes only finitely many values of $M$. These choices make $q$ a power of two and give $hM=q-1$, so the nonzero field elements can be partitioned into exactly $M$ blocks of $h$ evaluation points each. The actual finite-length code rate is $\frac KN=R+\calO(1/N)$, so $R$ denotes the limiting target rate rather than the exact finite-length rate. Use the canonical representation of $\F_q$ and primitive element $\gamma\in\F_q^\times$. Define $\alpha_{i,j} \coloneq  \gamma^{ih+j}$ for $i\in[M]$ and $j \in[h]$.
As $i$ and $j$ vary, these points enumerate $1,\gamma,\ldots,\gamma^{N-1}$, and hence all nonzero elements of $\F_q$ exactly once.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
\resizebox{0.9\linewidth}{!}{\input{pictures/block_construction.tex}}
\caption{From block max-LINSAT to folded OPI. For a general linear encoder $B$, the objective counts the accepted blocks $B_ix\in A_i$. Choosing Vandermonde rows at distinct evaluation points identifies $x$ with the coefficients of $Q(Y)=\sum_{r=0}^{K-1}x_rY^r$, so $B_ix=(Q(\alpha_{i,j}))_{j=0}^{h-1}$. This gives folded polynomial intersection: one membership query tests an entire $h$-vector of polynomial evaluations. The lower panel records our concrete family parameters for admissible $M$. Each acceptance set contains exactly half of the block symbols; the score is the fraction of blocks accepted by the chosen polynomial. The output is shown with its folded grouping, and dimensions are schematic.}
\label{fig:folded-matrix-shapes}
\end{figure}

Put $\Sigma\coloneq \F_q^h$. For a polynomial $Q\in\F_q[Y]$ with $\deg Q<K$, its $i$th folded evaluation is
\begin{align}
 c_i(Q)
 &\coloneq 
 \bigl(Q(\gamma^{ih+j})\bigr)_{j\in[h]}
 \in
 \Sigma.
\end{align}
The resulting folded Reed--Solomon code is
\begin{align}
 C_{\rm FRS}
 \coloneq 
 \operatorname{FRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K,h} = 
 \left\{
 (c_i(Q))_{i\in[M]}:
 Q\in\F_q[Y],\ \deg Q<K
 \right\}
 \subseteq
 \Sigma^M,
 \label{eq:concrete-frs-code}
\end{align}
where $\boldsymbol\alpha=(\gamma^t)_{t\in[N]}$.

The block alphabet has even cardinality $|\Sigma|=q^h=\exp(\Theta(M\log M))$, with each symbol represented using $h\log_2 q=\Theta(M\log M)$ bits. The acceptance sets are accessed through membership oracles, and all running-time bounds are measured in $M$.
Every block accepts exactly $|\Sigma|/2$ symbols. Thus balance is exact at every admissible size, with no rounding of the acceptance density.

\Cref{fig:folded-matrix-shapes} illustrates the specialization of block max-LINSAT to folded OPI and the parameters of our balanced membership-oracle model. Formally, we define balanced folded OPI as follows:

\begin{definition}[Balanced folded OPI]
\label{def:folded-opi}
For the folded Reed--Solomon data above, an instance is a tuple $\mathcal A=(A_i)_{i\in[M]}$ with $A_i\subseteq\Sigma$ and $\abs{A_i}=|\Sigma|/2$ for every $i$, accessed through the oracle defined in \eqref{eq:general-membership-oracle}.

A feasible output is a polynomial $Q\in\F_q[Y]$ of degree less than $K$, represented by its canonical length-$K$ coefficient vector.
Its score is the fraction of blocks whose evaluation symbols belong to their acceptance sets:
\begin{align}
\scor(Q) &\coloneq \scor(c(Q)) =\frac1M\sum_{i\in[M]} \mathds{1}_{\{c_i(Q)\in A_i\}}.
\end{align}
The threshold and multiplicative-approximation tasks use \cref{def:folded-max-linsat}. In particular, a score threshold $\beta$ requires at least $\ceil{\beta M}$ accepted blocks.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Concrete separation theorem}
For the balanced folded-OPI family and parameter choices above, the following theorem proves that DQI exceeds the asymptotically optimal score attainable with polynomially many classical queries under the sampled-oracle distribution. This establishes a provable quantum advantage in this membership-oracle setting.

\begin{theorem}[Quantum--classical query separation for folded OPI]
\label{thm:frs-separation}
Fix a rational rate $R\in(0,1)$ and the balanced folded-OPI family above. Define
\begin{align}
\alpha_{\rm C}(R)
&\coloneq\frac{1+R}{2},
&
\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)
&\coloneq\frac{1+\sqrt{R(2-R)}}2.
\label{eq:concrete-score-values}
\end{align}

We identify every valid coefficient-vector output with its polynomial. We write $A(\omega)$ for the polynomial output of a classical algorithm $A$ with internal random tape $\omega$, suppressing dependence on $M$ and $\mathcal A$.
In parts \textup{(ii)} and \textup{(iii)}, the sampled-oracle distribution chooses the sets $A_i$ independently and uniformly among the half-size subsets of $\Sigma$.
The following statements hold for all sufficiently large admissible $M$, with limits taken through admissible values.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item There is an explicit DQI algorithm running in quantum polynomial time and using one quantum query to each fixed-block membership oracle $\mathcal O_{A_i}$, hence at most $M$ queries in total. For every promised $\mathcal A$, its output satisfies
    \begin{align}
    \E\bigl[\scor(Q_{\rm DQI})\bigr]
    &=\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)+o(1),
    \label{eq:frs-dqi-value}
    \end{align}
    uniformly over $\mathcal A$. It uses a conventional generalized Reed--Solomon unique decoder strictly below half the minimum distance of the unfolded scalar dual code.

    \item There is a randomized polynomial-time implementation of Prange's information-set algorithm using $\calO(M)$ classical membership queries in the worst case such that
    \begin{align}
    \scor(Q_{\rm Prange})
    &\xrightarrow{\Prb}\alpha_{\rm C}(R).
    \label{eq:frs-prange}
    \end{align}
    The convergence is in probability over the sampled instance and the algorithm's randomness.

    \item Under the same sampled-oracle distribution, for every fixed $0<\eta<1-\alpha_{\rm C}(R)$, there are constants $\theta_{R,\eta},c_{R,\eta}>0$ and $M_0=M_0(R,\eta)$ such that, for every admissible $M\geq M_0$, every randomized classical algorithm $A$ making at most
    $T_M\leq\exp(\theta_{R,\eta}M/\log M)$ queries in the worst case satisfies
    \begin{align}
    \Prb\left[
    \scor\bigl(A(\omega)\bigr)
    \geq\alpha_{\rm C}(R)+\eta
    \right]&\leq e^{-c_{R,\eta}M}.
    \label{eq:frs-classical-lower}
    \end{align}
    The algorithm may use unlimited computation and arbitrary finite advice, provided its description and advice are fixed before $\mathcal A$ is sampled. The probability is over the sampled oracle $\mathcal A$ and the algorithm's random tape $\omega$, which is independent of $\mathcal A$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Part~\textup{(i)} is proved in \cref{sec:proof-quantum}, with full details in \cref{app:dqi}; parts~\textup{(ii)} and \textup{(iii)} are proved in \cref{sec:proof-prange,sec:proof-classical}.
\end{proof}

For every rate $R\in(0,1)$, the quantum score $\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)$ strictly exceeds the classical benchmark $\alpha_{\rm C}(R)$.

\paragraph{From expected score to verified threshold search.}
\Cref{thm:frs-separation}\textup{(i)} guarantees an expected score. To obtain an algorithm that reaches a certain score with bounded error, we first lower-bound the probability that a single DQI run reaches that score.
For any random variable $X\in[0,1]$ and threshold $\beta<\E[X]$, $\Prb[X\geq\beta] \geq \frac{\E[X]-\beta}{1-\beta}$. Fix a rational $\beta\in(\alpha_{\rm C}(R),\alpha_{\rm Q}(R))$. By \eqref{eq:frs-dqi-value}, for all sufficiently large admissible $M$,
\begin{align}
\Prb\left[
\scor(Q_{\rm DQI})\geq\beta
\right]
&\geq
\frac{\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)-\beta}{2(1-\beta)}.
\label{eq:expectation-to-search-concrete}
\end{align}
uniformly over every promised $\mathcal A$. Thus one DQI run succeeds with positive constant probability. A candidate can be verified using $M$ membership queries, so a constant number of verified repetitions raises the success probability to at least $2/3$ using $\calO_{R,\beta}(M)$ queries. If no candidate passes verification within the prescribed number of attempts, the algorithm reports failure.

\paragraph{Score and multiplicative approximation.}
To express the separation in terms of multiplicative approximation, we must relate the achieved scores to the optimum of each instance.
Since the optimum is at most one, the verified quantum threshold guarantee above also gives a $\beta$-approximation on every promised oracle. For the classical direction, a large approximation ratio need not imply a large score when the instance optimum is small. We therefore use \cref{lem:typical-perfect-satisfiability}, which shows that $\operatorname{OPT}=1$ except with doubly exponentially small probability under the sampled-oracle distribution. On the event $\operatorname{OPT}=1$, approximation ratio and score coincide, so the classical score query lower bound transfers to approximation.

\begin{corollary}[Ratio separation for DQI]
\label{cor:typical-dqi-ratio-separation}
Fix a rational rate $R\in(0,1)$ and a rational $r$ satisfying $\alpha_{\rm C}(R)<r<\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)$.
There is a DQI algorithm using $\calO_{R,r}(M)$ queries and quantum polynomial time. For all sufficiently large admissible $M$ and every promised oracle $\mathcal A$, the algorithm returns with probability $\geq 2/3$ a polynomial $Q_{{\rm DQI},r}$ with $\operatorname{ratio}(Q_{{\rm DQI},r})\geq r$. There also exist constants $\theta_{R,r},c_{R,r}>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large admissible $M$, every classical algorithm $A$ satisfying the hypotheses from \cref{thm:frs-separation}\textup{(iii)} with $T_M\leq\exp(\theta_{R,r}M/\log M)$ satisfies
\begin{align}
\Prb\left[
\operatorname{ratio}\bigl(A(\omega)\bigr)\geq r
\right]
&\leq e^{-c_{R,r}M}.
\label{eq:typical-dqi-classical-ratio-bound}
\end{align}
Prange's algorithm from \cref{thm:frs-separation}\textup{(ii)} achieves an approximation ratio converging in probability to $\alpha_{\rm C}(R)$.
The quantum success probability is over the algorithm's randomness for each fixed promised oracle. The classical probability and the Prange convergence are over the sampled oracle and the algorithm's independent randomness.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
See \cref{app:ordinary-dqi-consequences}.
\end{proof}

\section{Proof of the concrete separation}
\label{sec:concrete-proof}

The three parts of \cref{thm:frs-separation} use complementary properties of the same code. First, we show that DQI achieves the limiting expected score $\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)$ using conventional unique decoding of the unfolded scalar dual (\cref{sec:proof-quantum}). Next, Prange's algorithm gives a classical algorithm whose score converges to $\alpha_{\rm C}(R)$ on sampled instances (\cref{sec:proof-prange}). Finally, we combine list recoverability with an analysis of adaptive queries to prove that exceeding $\alpha_{\rm C}(R)$ by any fixed amount with constant probability requires super-polynomially many queries (\cref{sec:proof-classical}).

\subsection{Part \textup{(i)}: quantum guarantee}
\label{sec:proof-quantum}

The central idea of DQI is to reduce the problem of approximating a max-LINSAT instance to coherent syndrome decoding for the dual linear code. Jordan et al.~\cite[Supplementary Theorem~1.1]{JordanEtAl2025DQI} prove the resulting semicircle score law for scalar max-LINSAT. Motivated by the connection to Yamakawa and Zhandry~\cite{YamakawaZhandry2024}, their Supplementary Sections~11.2--11.4 construct the corresponding folded DQI states and the implementation of the required oracle. They do not, however, state the corresponding semicircle score law for folded predicates. 

For our concrete application, two points therefore remain to be verified. First, we show that the semicircle calculation extends to balanced folded predicates and that the required states can be prepared directly from our membership oracles. Second, we show that the resulting decoding problem can be solved efficiently for our folded Reed--Solomon family.

\paragraph{Extension of the semicircle law to folded codes.}
Fix an integer $M$ and consider a balanced folded max-LINSAT instance specified by an encoder $B$ with block rows $B_i$, $i\in[M]$, and acceptance sets $\mathcal A=(A_i)_{i\in[M]}$. Define the signed objective
\begin{align}
f(x)
&\coloneq
\sum_{i\in[M]}
\left(2\cdot \mathds 1_{\{B_i x\in A_i\}}-1\right).
\label{eq:folded-signed-objective}
\end{align}
Thus $f(x)$ is the number of accepted blocks minus the number of rejected blocks.\footnote{Since $\scor(Bx)=1/2+f(x)/(2M)$, maximizing the score is equivalent to maximizing $f(x)$.} The exact-balance promise implies that each local sign predicate has zero average over the block alphabet.

In this language, DQI reduces the task of finding a high-scoring candidate to unique decoding for the dual code $C^\perp=\ker B^T$, in syndrome form. More precisely, suppose that every label $e$ of block weight at most $\ell$ can be recovered from its syndrome $B^Te$. This decoding must be implemented coherently, since DQI applies it to a superposition of syndromes. Given an efficient coherent decoder and efficiently preparable weights, DQI prepares an approximation to the normalized state
\begin{align}
\ket{P(f)}
&\propto
\sum_{x\in\F_q^K}P(f(x))\ket{x},
\label{eq:main-dqi-filter-state}
\end{align}
where $P$ is a nonzero real polynomial of degree at most $\ell$. Appendix~\ref{app:dqi} supplies an explicit asymptotically-optimal choice for that polynomial and proves preparation to inverse-polynomial accuracy. Measuring this state in the computational basis returns a candidate $x$ with probability proportional to $\abs{P(f(x))}^2$. The filter polynomial $P$ can therefore be chosen to bias the output toward candidates of large score.

The same parameter $\ell$ controls both the strength of this bias and the required decoding radius: a degree-$\ell$ filter produces errors supported on at most $\ell$ blocks. Increasing $\ell$ permits a stronger bias toward high scores, but requires correcting more block errors and therefore makes the decoding problem harder.

\begin{proposition}[Balanced folded-DQI semicircle law]
\label{prop:folded-dqi-from-bdd}
Consider a family of balanced folded max-LINSAT objectives $f$ as above, indexed by the number $M$ of blocks, and define $C^\perp \coloneq\ker B^T$. Given a polynomial filter $P$ of degree at most $\ell$, let $\langle\scor\rangle_P$ denote the expected score of the candidate obtained by measuring the corresponding DQI state $\ket{P(f)}$ in the computational basis. Suppose
\begin{align}
2\ell+1
&<d_{\rm blk}(C^\perp).
\label{eq:folded-dqi-collision-condition}
\end{align}
In the limit $M\to\infty$, with $\ell/M\to\mu\in[0,1/2]$, the optimal choice of a degree-at-most-$\ell$ filter $P$ yields
\begin{align}
\langle\scor\rangle_P
&=
\frac12+\sqrt{\mu(1-\mu)}+o(1)
\label{eq:folded-dqi-semicircle}
\end{align}
where the $o(1)$ is uniform over all balanced acceptance-set collections.

\end{proposition}

The condition $2\ell+1<d_{\rm blk}(C^\perp)$ ensures that $C^\perp$ can in principle be decoded from up to $\ell$ worst-case block errors. If this unique decoding of up to $\ell$ errors can be implemented efficiently and coherently, then the corresponding DQI algorithm is efficient as well.

We prove the proposition in \cref{app:dqi}, where we also give an explicit asymptotically optimal filter and implement the folded DQI state from membership-oracle access. The proposition may therefore be treated as a black box here: the only remaining task is to verify the distance condition and supply an efficient coherent decoder.

\paragraph{Decoding the folded Reed--Solomon dual.}
Recall from \eqref{eq:frs-complete-block-count} that $J\coloneq\floor{K/h}=\floor{RM}$, and set $\ell_M\coloneq\floor{(J-1)/2}$. We verify separately that this choice satisfies the distance condition in the proposition and that the corresponding decoder is efficient.

For our encoder $B$ with folded-Vandermonde structure, the primal folded code is $C=C_{\rm{FRS}} = \operatorname{Fold}_h(\operatorname{RS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K})$. After unfolding, the dual of $\operatorname{RS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K}$ is the generalized Reed--Solomon code $\operatorname{GRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,\boldsymbol\nu,N-K}$, with $\boldsymbol\nu$ as defined in \eqref{eq:rs-dual-multipliers}. Therefore, by \eqref{eq:rs-dual-grs} and \eqref{eq:folded-rs-dual-grs},
\begin{align}
{C}^\perp
&=
\operatorname{Fold}_h\left(
\bigl(\operatorname{RS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,K}\bigr)^\perp
\right)
=
\operatorname{Fold}_h\left(
\operatorname{GRS}_{\boldsymbol\alpha,\boldsymbol\nu,N-K}
\right).
\end{align}
The unfolded generalized Reed--Solomon code has minimum distance $K+1$. A codeword supported on $b$ folded blocks has unfolded scalar weight at most $hb$. Since every nonzero word in the unfolded dual has scalar weight at least $K+1$, every nonzero word in $C^\perp$ must have block weight at least
\begin{align}
\ceil{\frac{K+1}{h}}
&=
\floor{\frac Kh}+1
=
J+1.
\end{align}
Hence $d_{\rm blk}(C^\perp)\geq J+1$. Moreover, our choice $\ell_M=\floor{(J-1)/2}$ gives $2\ell_M+1\leq J$. Combining these two bounds,
\begin{align}
2\ell_M+1
&\leq J
<J+1
\leq d_{\rm blk}(C^\perp).
\label{eq:main-folded-collision-check}
\end{align}
This verifies the distance condition \eqref{eq:folded-dqi-collision-condition} in \cref{prop:folded-dqi-from-bdd}.

It remains to implement the decoder. An error pattern affecting at most $\ell_M$ folded blocks produces at most $h\ell_M$ scalar errors after unfolding. Since
$2\ell_M\leq J-1$,
\begin{align}
h\ell_M
&\leq
\frac{h(J-1)}2
<
\frac{K+1}{2}.
\end{align}
The unfolded generalized Reed--Solomon code has minimum distance $K+1$, so the required number of scalar errors is strictly below half the minimum distance. It can therefore be corrected efficiently by conventional generalized Reed--Solomon unique decoding~\cite{Berlekamp1968,Massey1969}. Thus the required decoder can be implemented efficiently and applied coherently in superposition, making the corresponding DQI algorithm efficient.

Since $J=\floor{RM}$ and $\ell_M=\floor{(J-1)/2}$, we have
\begin{align}
\frac{\ell_M}{M}
&=
\frac R2+\calO\left(\frac1M\right)
\rightarrow\frac R2.
\end{align} 
For our folded Reed--Solomon instance, the measured candidate is the coefficient vector of a polynomial; denote this output polynomial by $Q_{\rm DQI}$. Applying \cref{prop:folded-dqi-from-bdd} with $\mu=R/2$ gives
\begin{align}
\E\left[
\scor\bigl(Q_{\rm DQI}\bigr)
\right]
&=
\frac{1+\sqrt{R(2-R)}}2+o(1)
=
\alpha_{\rm Q}(R)+o(1),
\end{align}
uniformly over every balanced instance. Together with the membership-oracle implementation from \cref{app:dqi}, this gives a quantum polynomial-time algorithm using one quantum query to each block-membership oracle in $(\mathcal O_{A_i})_{i\in [M]}$ and hence at most $M$ queries in total. This concludes the main argument underlying \cref{thm:frs-separation}\textup{(i)}, full details are given in \cref{app:dqi}.

