\section{Cleaning}
\label{sec:RG-reduction}

As suggested by Definition \ref{def:level-map}, we must consider the $Z$-sector cleaning $f$ of $\Gamma$ and compute the contraction $\kappa$ and fill bound $c_f$.

In \cite{balasubramanian2026passive}, the authors showed that if $m=2$, then for valid syndromes $\sigma\in Z(\Gamma)$, a layer-local cleaning procedure $f$ can be constructed to obtain the coarse-grained valid syndromes $\desc \sigma\in Z(C)$ such that
\begin{equation}
    |\desc \sigma|\le \kappa |\sigma|,\quad  \kappa =\frac{1}{2}
\end{equation}
Since the aim of \cite{balasubramanian2026passive} was to prove self-correction, which only deals with physical errors, $m=2$ and $\kappa <1$ are sufficient to derive an RG argument.
However, as suggested by the noisy contraction in Remark \ref{rem:setup}, when dealing with both physical and measurement errors, we further require the reduction factor to be $\kappa<1/2$.
By Remark \ref{rem:m-sheets}, one way of accomplishing this is to use the conventional $m=2$ sheet construction and group multiple (two) levels together as a single pass.
However, since it is more natural to have a single construction, we shall extend the argument to $m=3$ and show that $\kappa <1/2$.
Note that some properties extend easily to general $m\ge 2$, while others are nontrivial, and thus we will emphasize the distinction.
Motivated by Definition \ref{def:gluing} and the Cleaning Lemma \cite{yuan2026unified}, we define
\begin{definition}
    Let $\sigma\ne 0$ be a syndrome in $Z(\Gamma)$ and a coboundary of $Y^v$ for $v=x,q,z$. Then the \textbf{relative (syndrome) coexpansion} for $\sigma$ is defined as
    \begin{equation}
        \|\sigma\|^{UV} = \min_{\delta^V f=\sigma} \frac{|g^{UV} f|}{|\delta^V f|}
    \end{equation}
    Further define the \textbf{relative (syndrome) coexpansion} $\zeta^{UV}$ as the maximum of $\|\sigma\|^{UV}$ over syndromes $\sigma$ which are also coboundaries  of $Y^v$.
\end{definition}

