    where the row cocomplexes are shown in in Fig. \ref{fig:Yx-q-block} and \ref{fig:Yx-z-block} and, in particular, given by the direct sum of $q$ \textbf{blocks} and $z$ \textbf{blocks}, respectively.
    Using either Mayer-Vietoris\footnote{Note that when using Mayer-Vietoris to compute the cohomology of the bottom row complex corresponding to Fig. \ref{fig:Yx-z-block}, one must take the transpose, since by definition, the intersection must be a well-defined complex.} or the Snake-Lemma, it's straightforward to check that the middle row complex has cohomology $(0,q\otimes E)$, while the bottom row complex has homology $(0,0,z\otimes E)$.
    Therefore, one can apply \cite{yuan2026unified} so that $Y^x$ (without meta-check 3-cells) is homotopically equivalent to the column cocomplex as shown below 
    \begin{equation}
